    "thm-rpm-h-constraint-consistency": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-rpm-h-regular-constraint-system",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_{\\omega}$ and let $(M^{2n},\\omega)$ be a smooth symplectic manifold. A finite family of smooth functions $\\chi_1,\\ldots,\\chi_r$ defined on an open neighborhood $U\\subseteq M$ is a **regular primary constraint family** on\n\n$$C:=\\{z\\in U:\\chi_1(z)=\\cdots=\\chi_r(z)=0\\}$$\n\nwhen $d\\chi_1(z),\\ldots,d\\chi_r(z)$ are linearly independent at every $z\\in C$. The regular-level theorem [[thm-a-regular-level-set-is-an-embedded-submanifold]] then gives $C$ its embedded-submanifold structure and $T_zC=\\bigcap_a\\ker d\\chi_a(z)$. For functions $F,G$ on $U$, write $F\\approx G$ when $F|_C=G|_C$; thus $F\\approx0$ means that $F$ vanishes on $C$, without requiring it to vanish off $C$.\n\nGiven a smooth canonical Hamiltonian $H_c$ on $U$ and multiplier functions $u^a(t)$, the **total generator** is\n\n$$H_T(t,z):=H_c(t,z)+\\sum_{a=1}^r u^a(t)\\chi_a(z).$$\n\nThe functions $u^a$ are independent parameter functions; the definition does not assert that their equations determine them. If $H_c$ is an energy and $\\chi_a$ has units $[\\chi_a]$, then $u^a$ has units energy divided by $[\\chi_a]$, so every term in $H_T$ has the same units. A later constraint, if generated by consistency, is not declared primary by this definition.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-rpm-h-canonical-poisson-and-flow",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $Q$ be a finite-dimensional smooth manifold as in [[def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space]], and write its cotangent phase space in induced coordinates $(q^i,p_i)$. With $\\omega=dq^i\\wedge dp_i$, define $X_H$ by $\\iota_{X_H}\\omega=dH$ and define the Poisson bracket by $\\{F,G\\}:=X_G(F)$. Then\n\n$$X_H=\\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial p_i}\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial q^i}-\\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial q^i}\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial p_i},\\qquad \\{F,G\\}=F_{q^i}G_{p_i}-F_{p_i}G_{q^i}.$$\n\nThe bracket is coordinate independent and makes smooth functions a Poisson algebra. If $H_t$ is smooth on an open time-dependent domain $D\\subset\\mathbb R\\times T^*Q$, each solution of $\\dot z=X_{H_t}(z)$ has local uniqueness and smooth dependence on initial data and parameters; on its actual interval,\n\n$$\\frac{d}{dt}F(t,z(t))=\\partial_tF+\\{F,H_t\\}.$$\n\nNo completeness or global flow is asserted. If $H$ is smooth, each local time-$t$ flow map preserves ω.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-darboux-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. For every point $p$ of a $2n$-dimensional\nsymplectic manifold $(M,\\omega)$, there are coordinates\n$(q^1,\\ldots,q^n,p_1,\\ldots,p_n)$ centred at $p$ in which\n\n$$\\omega=\\sum_{i=1}^n dq^i\\wedge dp_i.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-cm-stationarity-iff-euler-lagrange",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(O,\\mathcal L)$ be a time-dependent Lagrangian chart over $[a,b]\\times U$ in the sense of\n[[def-cm-time-dependent-lagrangian-and-action]] and let $q:[a,b]\\to U$ be a $C^2$ admissible curve.\nThen $q$ is stationary for the action $S$ if and only if\n\n$$E_{\\mathcal L}(q)(t)=0\\qquad\\text{for every }t\\in[a,b].$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "In a Darboux chart, the integrand is $p_i\\dot q^i-H_c-u^a\\chi_a$. Stationarity under independent variations of $u^a$ gives $\\chi_a=0$: apply [F4] to the combined coordinates $(q,p,u)$, whose integrand has no $\\dot u$ term, using its fixed-endpoint subclass. Stationarity under fixed-endpoint variations of $p_i,q^i$ gives $\\dot q^i=\\partial_{p_i}H_T$ and $\\dot p_i=-\\partial_{q^i}H_T$, where $H_T=H_c+u^a\\chi_a$. The equations hold on $[a,b]$, including endpoints by continuity.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The multiplier functions depend on λ only, so $d_zH_T=dH_c+u^a d\\chi_a$. By linearity of $\\iota_{X_H}\\omega=dH$, $X_{H_T}=X_{H_c}+u^aX_{\\chi_a}$. If instead phase-dependent extensions $u^a(z,\\lambda)$ are used, the extra term $\\chi_aX_{u^a}$ vanishes on $N$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "Differentiate $\\chi_a(\\lambda,z(\\lambda))=0$ along a solution; the chain rule yields $0=\\partial_\\lambda\\chi_a+d_z\\chi_a(\\dot z)$.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Substitute the vector field from step 2.1 and use [F2]: $d_z\\chi_a(X_{H_c})=\\{\\chi_a,H_c\\}$ and $d_z\\chi_a(X_{\\chi_b})=\\{\\chi_a,\\chi_b\\}$, giving the displayed consistency equation at each point of the trajectory. Requiring the total vector field to preserve every initial point of the moving constraint surface requires this equation throughout $N_\\lambda$. Its possible algebraic outcomes depend on its coefficient matrix and remaining constraints; no rank or termination premise was assumed.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 2.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
