    "thm-rpm-h-first-class-local-reduction": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-rpm-h-regular-constraint-system",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_{\\omega}$ and let $(M^{2n},\\omega)$ be a smooth symplectic manifold. A finite family of smooth functions $\\chi_1,\\ldots,\\chi_r$ defined on an open neighborhood $U\\subseteq M$ is a **regular primary constraint family** on\n\n$$C:=\\{z\\in U:\\chi_1(z)=\\cdots=\\chi_r(z)=0\\}$$\n\nwhen $d\\chi_1(z),\\ldots,d\\chi_r(z)$ are linearly independent at every $z\\in C$. The regular-level theorem [[thm-a-regular-level-set-is-an-embedded-submanifold]] then gives $C$ its embedded-submanifold structure and $T_zC=\\bigcap_a\\ker d\\chi_a(z)$. For functions $F,G$ on $U$, write $F\\approx G$ when $F|_C=G|_C$; thus $F\\approx0$ means that $F$ vanishes on $C$, without requiring it to vanish off $C$.\n\nGiven a smooth canonical Hamiltonian $H_c$ on $U$ and multiplier functions $u^a(t)$, the **total generator** is\n\n$$H_T(t,z):=H_c(t,z)+\\sum_{a=1}^r u^a(t)\\chi_a(z).$$\n\nThe functions $u^a$ are independent parameter functions; the definition does not assert that their equations determine them. If $H_c$ is an energy and $\\chi_a$ has units $[\\chi_a]$, then $u^a$ has units energy divided by $[\\chi_a]$, so every term in $H_T$ has the same units. A later constraint, if generated by consistency, is not declared primary by this definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-symplectic-form-and-symplectic-manifold",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **symplectic form** on a smooth manifold $M$ is a smooth two-form $\\omega$\nsuch that\n\n1. $d\\omega=0$, and\n2. $(T_pM,\\omega_p)$ is a [[def-symplectic-vector-space|symplectic vector\n   space]] for every $p\\in M$.\n\nThe pair $(M,\\omega)$ is a **symplectic manifold**. Thus both closedness and\npointwise nondegeneracy are required. A zero-dimensional manifold with its\nzero two-form satisfies the definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-symplectic-orthogonal-complement",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(V,\\omega)$ be a [[def-symplectic-vector-space|symplectic vector space]]\nand $W\\le V$. Its **symplectic orthogonal complement** is\n\n$$W^\\omega=\\{v\\in V:\\omega(v,w)=0\\text{ for every }w\\in W\\}.$$\n\nEquivalently, $W^\\omega=(\\omega^\\flat)^{-1}(\\operatorname{ann}W)$, so it is a\nlinear subspace. In particular, $0^\\omega=V$ and $V^\\omega=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "prop-symplectic-double-orthogonal-and-dimension-identities",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $W$ is a subspace of the finite-dimensional symplectic vector space\n$(V,\\omega)$, then\n\n$$\\dim W+\\dim W^\\omega=\\dim V,\\qquad (W^\\omega)^\\omega=W.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-smooth-dependence-of-ode-solutions-on-parameters",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $F(t,x,\\lambda)$ be jointly $C^\\infty$ in $(t,x,\\lambda)$ on an open time-state-parameter\ndomain. Near any base data $(t_0,x_0,\\lambda_0)$ there are a compact time\ninterval $I$ and a neighbourhood $W$ of $(x_0,\\lambda_0)$ such that, for every\n$(y,\\lambda)\\in W$, the solution of\n\n$$x'(t)=F(t,x(t),\\lambda),\\qquad x(t_0)=y,$$\n\nis defined on $I$, and the resulting solution map is smooth in the pair\n$(y,\\lambda)$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-frobenius-local-coordinate-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal D$ be a rank-$k$ smooth distribution on an $n$-manifold $M$. Then\nthe following are equivalent:\n\n1. $\\mathcal D$ is integrable.\n2. $\\mathcal D$ is involutive.\n\nWhen these conditions hold, every point $p \\in M$ has a coordinate\nneighborhood $(x^1,\\dots,x^n)$ in which\n\n$$ \\mathcal D = \\operatorname{span}\\!\\left(\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x^1},\\dots,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x^k}\\right). $$",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-rpm-h-canonical-poisson-and-flow",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $Q$ be a finite-dimensional smooth manifold as in [[def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space]], and write its cotangent phase space in induced coordinates $(q^i,p_i)$. With $\\omega=dq^i\\wedge dp_i$, define $X_H$ by $\\iota_{X_H}\\omega=dH$ and define the Poisson bracket by $\\{F,G\\}:=X_G(F)$. Then\n\n$$X_H=\\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial p_i}\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial q^i}-\\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial q^i}\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial p_i},\\qquad \\{F,G\\}=F_{q^i}G_{p_i}-F_{p_i}G_{q^i}.$$\n\nThe bracket is coordinate independent and makes smooth functions a Poisson algebra. If $H_t$ is smooth on an open time-dependent domain $D\\subset\\mathbb R\\times T^*Q$, each solution of $\\dot z=X_{H_t}(z)$ has local uniqueness and smooth dependence on initial data and parameters; on its actual interval,\n\n$$\\frac{d}{dt}F(t,z(t))=\\partial_tF+\\{F,H_t\\}.$$\n\nNo completeness or global flow is asserted. If $H$ is smooth, each local time-$t$ flow map preserves ω.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For $w\\in T_zN$, [F1] gives $d\\chi_a(w)=0$, hence $\\omega(X_{\\chi_a},w)=0$ and each $X_{\\chi_a}\\in(T_zN)^\\omega$. The $d\\chi_a$ are independent, so their images under the isomorphism $\\omega^\\flat$ are independent; therefore these $r$ vectors are independent. The level has codimension $r$, and [F4] gives $\\dim(T_zN)^\\omega=r$, so they span the symplectic orthogonal.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "On $N$, $d\\chi_b(X_{\\chi_a})=\\{\\chi_b,\\chi_a\\}=0$ by first-classness and [F7], so each $X_{\\chi_a}$ is tangent. Their independence from step 1.1 implies $r\\le\\dim N=2n-r$, hence $r\\le n$. The radical of the restricted form is $T_zN\\cap(T_zN)^\\omega$; since the orthogonal is already spanned by tangent $X_{\\chi_a}$, this intersection is exactly that span.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F7",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For smooth kernel fields $U,V$ and any tangent field $W$, apply $d\\omega_N=0$ to $(U,V,W)$. All terms containing contractions with $U$ or $V$ vanish, leaving $\\omega_N([U,V],W)=0$. Thus ([U,V]) is again in the kernel and the distribution is involutive.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "The Frobenius frame proof solves a transverse linear matrix ODE to straighten a local frame. Its coefficients are smooth, so [F5] gives a smooth local solution in transverse coordinates; the initial matrix is identity, so its determinant remains nonzero after shrinking the coordinate neighborhood. The resulting frame is involutive and [F6] supplies local $(s,y)$ coordinates. This explicitly supplies the parameter dependence needed in that construction.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "Since every $\\omega_N$-kernel vector is tangent to the $s$-leaves, the contraction of $\\omega_N$ with $\\partial_{s^a}$ vanishes. Therefore its coordinate expression has no $ds^a$ factor.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "claim": "In the Frobenius chart, step 5.1 writes $\\omega_N=\\tfrac12\\sum_{i,j}\\omega_{ij}(s,y)\\,dy^i\\wedge dy^j$. Since $d\\omega_N=0$, the coefficient of each independent form $ds^a\\wedge dy^i\\wedge dy^j$ with $i<j$ is $\\partial_{s^a}\\omega_{ij}=0$. If the $y$-space has dimension less than two, the displayed two-form is zero and is already independent of $s$. Thus the coefficients define a form $\\omega_{\\mathrm{red}}$ on the local $y$-space, and the coordinate projection pulls it back to $\\omega_N$; the remaining $dy$-components of $d\\omega_N=0$ show $d\\omega_{\\mathrm{red}}=0$. Its kernel would lift to a vector in $TN$ orthogonal to all of $TN$, hence to the already-quotiented kernel, so $\\omega_{\\mathrm{red}}$ is nondegenerate.",
          "step": "6.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "step 4.1",
            "step 5.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
