    "thm-rpm-h-local-legendre-and-energy-descent": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-rpm-h-almost-regular-legendre",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice ACω, as in [[def-countable-choice]], for the smooth cotangent-bundle carrier convention used by this page; no full Axiom of Choice is assumed.\n\nLet $\\mathcal D$ be an open subset of the time-dependent tangent bundle $\\mathbb R\\times TQ$ of a finite-dimensional smooth manifold $Q$, and let $L:\\mathcal D\\to\\mathbb R$ be smooth. For fixed $t$, the **fibre derivative** is the map\n\n$$\\mathbb F L(t,q,v)=(t,q,p),\\qquad p(w)=\\left.\\frac{d}{ds}\\right|_{s=0}L(t,q,v+sw),$$\n\nas in [[def-fibre-derivative-and-legendre-transform-of-a-lagrangian]]. In induced coordinates $p_i=\\partial L/\\partial v^i$.\n\nThe Lagrangian is **regular** at $(t,q,v)$ when the velocity Hessian $(\\partial^2L/\\partial v^i\\partial v^j)$ is invertible there; regularity gives only a local inverse branch. It is **hyperregular on a declared domain** when $\\mathbb F L$ is a diffeomorphism from that entire domain onto an open subset of $\\mathbb R\\times T^*Q$.\n\nIt is **almost regular on a declared open domain $\\mathcal D_0\\subseteq\\mathcal D$**, with the inherited open-submanifold structure, when the following are all assumed: $\\mathbb F L|_{\\mathcal D_0}$ has constant rank; its image $N$ is an embedded submanifold of $\\mathbb R\\times T^*Q$; its corestriction $\\mathcal D_0\\to N$ is a surjective submersion (its differential is surjective at every point); and every fibre of that corestriction is connected. These hypotheses are not consequences of singularity alone.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-fibre-derivative-and-legendre-transform-of-a-lagrangian",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a smooth Lagrangian $L:TQ\\to\\mathbb R$, its **fibre derivative** or\n**Legendre map** is the fibre-preserving smooth map\n\n$$\\mathbb FL:TQ\\to T^*Q,\\qquad (\\mathbb FL(q,v))(w)=\\left.\\frac d{ds}\\right|_{s=0}L(q,v+sw).$$\n\nIn bundle coordinates it is\n$\\mathbb FL(q^i,v^i)=(q^i,p_i)$ with\n$p_i=\\partial L/\\partial v^i$. This coordinate formula also shows smoothness\nand that the base point $q$ is unchanged.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "cor-local-normal-form-for-submersions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $F:M^m\\to N^n$ be a smooth submersion at $p\\in M$. Then there are charts\nnear $p$ and $F(p)$ in which the coordinate representative of $F$ is\n\n$$ (u,v)\\longmapsto u $$\n\nnear the distinguished point.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "A tangent vector to a fibre of $\\mathbb FL$ has the form $(0,0,\\delta v)$ with $L_{v^iv^j}\\delta v^j=0$, since the $(t,q)$ components of $\\mathbb FL$ are identity components and its $p$-variation vanishes. Differentiating $E_L$ in this direction gives $dE_L(\\delta v)=v^iL_{v^iv^j}\\delta v^j=0$; the $L_{v^j}\\delta v^j$ terms cancel.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "On a connected fibre, a smooth function whose differential vanishes is constant: it is locally constant in coordinate neighborhoods, and a locally constant function on a connected space is constant. Hence $E_L$ is constant on each fibre of $\\mathbb FL$. Define $h(y)$ to be this common value for any point above $y\\in N$; surjectivity gives existence, and constancy gives well-definedness and uniqueness.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For each $y\\in N$, [F3] gives a smooth local section $s$ of $\\mathbb FL$. On that neighborhood $h=E_L\\circ s$, so $h$ is smooth. These local formulas agree on overlaps because the value is already uniquely constant on each fibre. Thus globally on $N$, $E_L=h\\circ\\mathbb FL$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F3",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
