        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a smooth four-manifold without boundary, so that $M$ is Hausdorff\nand second countable and carries a maximal smooth atlas\n([[def-smooth-manifold]]). Let $g$ be a smooth covariant $2$-tensor field on $M$\nin the sense of [[def-gr-smooth-covariant-two-tensor-field]]. Then $g$ assigns\nto every $p\\in M$ a bilinear form $g_p:T_pM\\times T_pM\\to\\mathbb R$\n([[def-derivation-at-a-point-and-tangent-space]]).\n\n**(a) Lorentzian metric.** The field $g$ is a **Lorentzian metric of signature\n$(-,+,+,+)$** on $M$ when it is symmetric and nondegenerate, and at every\n$p\\in M$ the pair $(T_pM,g_p)$ is a Lorentz vector space of signature\n$(-,+,+,+)$ in the sense of [[def-lorentz-form-and-inertia]]: that is, $g_p$ has\ninertia $(3,1,0)$, so that some — equivalently every — basis of $T_pM$ in which\nthe matrix of $g_p$ is diagonal has exactly one negative and three positive\ndiagonal entries. The pair $(M,g)$ is a **smooth Lorentzian four-manifold** (or\nLorentzian manifold of signature $(-,+,+,+)$). In a smooth chart $(U,x)$ the\n**components** of $g$ are the $C^\\infty$ functions\n$g_{ab}(q)=g_q(\\partial_a|_q,\\partial_b|_q)$ of\n[[def-gr-smooth-covariant-two-tensor-field]], and $g_{ab}=g_{ba}$ by symmetry.\nWe use indices $a,b,c,d=0,1,2,3$ and the Einstein summation convention, with\ncomponents in a chart written as lower-case indexed symbols and equations\nbetween indexed fields understood basis-independently. These conventions are\nfixed once for this library and the physical reading of the symbols is recorded\nwith the framework postulates, separately from this mathematical definition.\n\n**(b) Pointwise causal structure.** For every $p\\in M$ the vocabulary of\n[[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]] applies to the Lorentz vector\nspace $(T_pM,g_p)$: a tangent vector $v\\in T_pM$ is **timelike** when\n$g_p(v,v)<0$, **null** when $v\\neq0$ and $g_p(v,v)=0$, **spacelike** when\n$g_p(v,v)>0$, and **causal** when it is timelike or null. Every nonzero\n$v\\in T_pM$ has exactly one causal character. The **timelike set**\n$T_p:=\\{v\\in T_pM:g_p(v,v)<0\\}$ has exactly two connected components, each\nnonempty, open, convex and path-connected; each $g_p$-orthonormal basis\n$e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3$ of $T_pM$ satisfies $g_p(e_a,e_b)=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)_{ab}$\nfor some ordering, so in particular nonzero orthonormal vectors exist at every\npoint ([[lem-lorentz-form-has-orthonormal-basis]],\n[[lem-timelike-cone-has-two-convex-components]]).\n\n**(c) Time orientation.** A **time orientation** of $(M,g)$, also called a\n**choice of future cone**, is a smooth vector field $N$ on $M$\n([[def-smooth-vector-field-as-a-tangent-bundle-section]], whose tangent-bundle\nsmooth structure uses the axiom $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ of\n[[def-countable-choice]]; this is the only use of that axiom in this\ndefinition) such that\n\n$$g_p(N_p,N_p)<0\\qquad\\text{for every }p\\in M .$$\n\nThe function $p\\mapsto g_p(N_p,N_p)$ is $C^\\infty$ because $g$ is a smooth\ncovariant $2$-tensor field and $N$ a smooth field, so the condition is a\npointwise strict inequality between a smooth function and $0$; since\n$g_p(N_p,N_p)<0$ implies $N_p\\neq0$, a time orientation is nowhere vanishing.\nA **time-oriented smooth Lorentzian four-manifold** is a triple $(M,g,N)$\nconsisting of a smooth Lorentzian four-manifold and a time orientation $N$.\n\nGiven a time orientation $N$ and $p\\in M$, the vector $N_p$ is timelike, and we\n**define** a nonzero causal vector $v\\in T_pM$ to be **future-directed** when\n$g_p(v,N_p)<0$ and **past-directed** when $g_p(v,N_p)>0$. The classification of\nnonzero causal vectors into exactly these two classes, and its agreement with\nthe two components of the timelike set (the future component is the component\n$C_p^+$ containing $N_p$, and the future causal vectors form\n$\\operatorname{cl}(C_p^+)\\setminus\\{0\\}$), is proved in\n[[lem-gr-future-causal-classification-by-a-timelike-field]]; in particular no\nnonzero causal $v$ satisfies $g_p(v,N_p)=0$, so the definition is exhaustive and\nmutually exclusive. Replacing $N$ by $-N$ reverses every future/past label, and\ntwo time orientations $N,N'$ determine the same future-directed causal vectors\nat every point where $g_p(N_p,N'_p)<0$, while they determine opposite ones\nwhere $g_p(N_p,N'_p)>0$, by\n[[lem-gr-timelike-fields-define-equal-or-opposite-future-cones]]; that lemma\nalso shows the sign of $p\\mapsto g_p(N_p,N'_p)$ is constant on every connected\ncomponent of $M$.\n\n**(d) The inverse metric.** Because $g_p$ is nondegenerate, it induces at each\n$p$ an isomorphism $\\flat_p:T_pM\\to T_p^*M$, $v\\mapsto g_p(v,\\cdot)$, and an\ninverse metric $g_p^{-1}$ on the dual space; the inverse metric, the musical\nisomorphisms and the components $g^{ab}$ are defined separately on this page,\nwhere the smoothness of those components is also proved. Neither construction\nuses any choice principle beyond the one already recorded in (c).\n\n\n**(e) Smooth timelike curves and their scalar normalization.** Before using\nthese quantities in the physical conventions, fix a positive real scaling\nparameter $c$ (with no physical meaning or dimension in this mathematical\nitem). A **smooth future timelike curve** is a smooth map $\\gamma:I\\to M$\nfrom a nonempty open real interval $I$ ([[def-interval]],\n[[def-c-r-and-smooth-maps-between-smooth-manifolds]]) whose velocity derivation\n$\\gamma'(s)\\in T_{\\gamma(s)}M$\n([[def-velocity-derivation-of-a-smooth-curve]]) satisfies\n$g(\\gamma',\\gamma')<0$ and $g(\\gamma',N)<0$ at every $s\\in I$.\nIn a chart its components are $d(x^a\\circ\\gamma)/ds$, $a=0,1,2,3$.\nFor a supplied base point $s_0\\in I$ and additive constant $\\tau_0\\in\\mathbb R$\ndefine\n\n$$h(s):=\\sqrt{-g_{\\gamma(s)}(\\gamma'(s),\\gamma'(s))},\\qquad \\tau(s):=\\tau_0+\\frac1c\\int_{s_0}^{s}h(r)\\,dr,\\qquad U(s):=\\frac{c\\gamma'(s)}{h(s)}.$$\n\nThe integral is oriented when $s<s_0$. The function $\\tau$ is called\n**proper time** for this scaling and $U$ the **four-velocity** of the smooth\ncurve; its increment times $c$ is the Lorentzian length. The well-definedness\nproof below shows $\\tau'>0$, that $\\tau:I\\to\\tau(I)$ is a smooth\ndiffeomorphism, and that $U$ is the velocity of $\\gamma\\circ\\tau^{-1}$.\nThus $g(U,U)=-c^2$ and $U$ is future timelike. This introduces the smooth\ncase before any physical unit reading; the later worldline definition extends\nit to its stated $C^k$, endpoint and piecewise-$C^1$ classes. It asserts no\nclock coupling, motion law or physical value of $c$.\n\n**Convention and scope.** The physical reading of a smooth time-oriented\nLorentzian four-manifold as a classical spacetime model, and the domain in which\nthat reading is adopted, are recorded in the framework postulate\n`post-gr-spacetime-and-classical-domain`; the words spacetime and event carry no\nmathematical content beyond this definition, and this item asserts no claim\nabout nature. The dimension $4$ and the absence of boundary are part of the\ndata; nothing in the definition assumes that $M$ is nonempty or connected, and\nno completeness, extendibility or maximality property of $(M,g)$ is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use the signature $(-,+,+,+)$ throughout. In the flat case, **Minkowski spacetime** means the affine model $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ of [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]]: $A$ is an affine space, $\\eta$ is a nondegenerate Lorentz form on its four-dimensional displacement space, and $C^+$ selects the future cone. In the curved case, a **background spacetime** means a prescribed smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold $(M,g,N)$ of [[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]; the metric and time orientation are fixed data, not dynamical unknowns here. No field equation or completeness assumption is included.\n\nA **massive worldline** on an open interval $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ is a $C^2$ curve $x:I\\to M$ with future-directed timelike tangent $T=dx/ds$ at every parameter value. This is the smooth subclass of [[def-gr-timelike-worldline-and-proper-time]]. A **null worldline** is a $C^2$ curve with nonzero future-directed null tangent everywhere. Its tangent has no proper-time normalization: $g(T,T)=0$, so the massive proper-time formula does not apply. In the flat model these are affine curves into $A$ with tangent in the selected future timelike or null cone from [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]].\n\nAn **observer at an event** $p\\in M$ is a future unit timelike vector $n\\in T_pM$ with $g_p(n,n)=-1$, as in [[def-gr-observer-and-rest-space]]. Its instantaneous rest space is $n^\\perp=\\{v:g_p(v,n)=0\\}$. This is a pointwise vector-space construction; the definition does not assign a clock reading or a moving frame to a lone observer.\n\nAn **observer congruence** on an open set $U\\subseteq M$ is a smooth field of future unit timelike vectors on $U$, as in [[def-gr-observer-congruence-and-adapted-charts]]; its observer curves are its integral curves. A **tetrad** is a chosen orthonormal frame field, as in [[def-gr-local-frame-and-tetrad-fields]]. A **chart** is a coordinate map; an adapted chart is a chart whose spatial coordinates are constant along the congruence's integral curves. These objects are distinct: a chart need not be inertial, a tetrad need not be coordinate induced, and a congruence is a field of observers rather than a chart or a frame. This distinction agrees with [[def-sr-chart-tetrad-and-congruence-distinction]].\n\nThe coordinates and units used later are SI: in an inertial chart $x^0=ct$ and $x^i$ are metres, $t$ is seconds, $c>0$ is metres per second, and proper time is measured in seconds. In a general chart, coordinate components inherit their coordinate units; invariant equations are tensorial. No global inertial chart, global tetrad, or global observer congruence is asserted to exist.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-a-regular-level-set-is-an-embedded-submanifold",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $F:M^m\\to N^n$ be smooth, let $q\\in N$ be a regular value, and assume\n$F^{-1}(q)$ is nonempty. Then $F^{-1}(q)$ is an embedded submanifold of\ncodimension $n$. Equivalently, it has dimension $m-n$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-rpm-h-homogeneous-legendre-degeneracy",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $L(q,v)$ be $C^2$ on a conic open subset of a tangent bundle, and suppose $L(q,av)=aL(q,v)$ for every $a>0$ in its domain. At each nonzero $v$,\n\n$$v^iL_{v^i}=L,\\qquad L_{v^iv^j}v^i=0,\\qquad E_L:=v^iL_{v^i}-L=0.$$\n\nConsequently the velocity Hessian is singular and the fibre derivative cannot be a local diffeomorphism there; a regular Legendre equivalence theorem cannot be applied. These conclusions do not imply that the motion is static or that the Lagrangian has a specified constraint surface.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-lorentz-form-has-orthonormal-basis",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\eta$ be a Lorentz form on a $4$-dimensional real vector space $V$. There\nis an ordered basis $(e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ of $V$ with\n$\\eta(e_a,e_b)=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)_{ab}$ for all $a,b$. Such a basis\nis called **$\\eta$-orthonormal**; in every $\\eta$-orthonormal ordered basis\nexactly one basis vector is timelike and the other three are spacelike, and the\ncount is the inertia $(3,1,0)$ of $\\eta$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $c > 0$ and let $(A,V,\\eta,C^{+})$ be an oriented time-oriented affine\nMinkowski space ([[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]]).\n\n**(a) Norm of the four-velocity.** Let $x : I \\to A$ be a $C^{1}$ future\ntimelike worldline with four-velocity $U$\n([[def-sr-four-velocity-and-four-acceleration]]). Then\n$\\eta(U(s),U(s)) = -c^{2}$ for every $s \\in I$; equivalently the dimensionless\nfour-velocity $u = U/c$ satisfies $\\eta(u,u) = -1$, and $U(s)$ is future\ntimelike. Thus the dimensionless four-velocity lies on the future unit\nhyperboloid $\\{v \\in C^{+}:\\eta(v,v)=-1\\}$.\n\n**(b) Orthogonality of the four-acceleration.** If $x$ is $C^{2}$ at $s$, then\n$\\eta(U(s),A(s)) = 0$: the four-acceleration has no component along the\nfour-velocity; if in addition $A(s) \\ne 0$, then $A(s)$ is spacelike.\n\n**(c) Rest space and projection.** Let $n \\in V$ be future unit timelike,\n$\\eta(n,n) = -1$, and put $n^{\\perp} := \\{\\,v \\in V : \\eta(n,v) = 0\\,\\}$\n([[def-lorentz-orthogonality-and-orthogonal-complement]]). Then $n^{\\perp}$ is a\n3-dimensional linear subspace of $V$, the restriction of $\\eta$ to $n^{\\perp}$\nis positive definite, and the map $h : V \\to V$, $h(v) := v + \\eta(n,v)\\,n$, is\nlinear, idempotent with $h \\circ h = h$, has image $n^{\\perp}$ and kernel\n$\\operatorname{span}(n)$, satisfies $h(n) = 0$ and $\\eta(n,h(v)) = 0$ for all\n$v \\in V$, and every $v \\in V$ has the unique decomposition\n$v = -\\eta(n,v)\\,n + h(v)$. Moreover $|w| := \\sqrt{\\eta(w,w)}$ defines a norm\non $n^{\\perp}$.\n\n**(d) Relative velocity and Lorentz factor.** Let $n$ and $u$ be future unit\ntimelike vectors, put $\\gamma := -\\eta(n,u)$ and $w := c\\,h(u)/\\gamma$. Then\n$\\gamma \\ge 1$, $w$ lies in $n^{\\perp}$,\n$u = \\gamma\\,(n + w/c)$, $|w|^{2} = c^{2}(1-\\gamma^{-2}) < c^{2}$,\n$\\gamma = (1-|w|^{2}/c^{2})^{-1/2}$, and $\\gamma = 1$ if and only if $u = n$.\nThe vector $w$ is the velocity of a worldline with four-velocity $cu$ relative\nto the observer $n$, and $\\gamma$ is its Lorentz factor.\n\n**(e) Coordinate form.** Let $(o;e_0,\\dots,e_3)$ be an inertial frame and let\n$y$ be a $C^{1}$ future timelike worldline whose inertial coordinates are\n$t \\mapsto (ct,r(t))$, with coordinate velocity $w(t) := r'(t)$ in\n$\\mathbb{R}^{3}$ and, when $y$ is $C^{2}$, coordinate acceleration\n$a(t) := w'(t)$. Then $|w(t)| < c$, the four-velocity is\n$U = \\gamma_w\\,(c,w)$ with $\\gamma_w := (1-|w|^{2}/c^{2})^{-1/2}$, and the\nfour-acceleration is $A = \\gamma_w\\,\\frac{d}{dt}\\bigl(\\gamma_w(c,w)\\bigr)$, so\nthat\n\n$$A^{0} = \\gamma_w^{4}\\,\\frac{w \\cdot a}{c}, \\qquad A_{\\mathrm{vec}} = \\gamma_w^{2}\\,a + \\gamma_w^{4}\\,\\frac{(w \\cdot a)\\,w}{c^{2}} ;$$\n\nconsequently\n$\\eta(A,A) = \\gamma_w^{4}|a|^{2} + \\gamma_w^{6}(w \\cdot a)^{2}/c^{2} \\ge 0$,\nwith $\\eta(A,A) = 0$ if and only if $a = 0$, and $\\eta(U,A) = 0$. For the\nobserver $n = e_0$ of the chart, clauses (c) and (d) specialize to\n$\\gamma = \\gamma_w$ and $w_{\\mathrm{rel}} = (0,w)$ in the frame coordinates, identified with $w$ via the spatial basis.\n\n**(f) Scope.** The statement is a theorem about the affine Minkowski model with\na fixed positive constant $c$; it asserts no clock reading and identifies no\nphysical four-velocity. No choice principle is used.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Use the supplied smooth tangent/cotangent bundle structure under [A1] and the Lorentzian data [F1]. Differentiate $L=-mc\\sqrt{-g_{ij}V^iV^j}$ with respect to $V^a$. Because $s>0$, ordinary chain rule gives $p_a=mcV_a/s$. Differentiating again gives $L_{V^aV^b}=mc(g_{ab}/s+V_aV_b/s^3)$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Contract the Hessian with $V^b$: $mc(V_a/s+V_a g(V,V)/s^3)=0$, since $g(V,V)=-s^2$. For $W\\perp_g V$, contraction with $W$ has quadratic value $(mc/s)g(W,W)$. The normalized vector $u=V/s$ is future unit timelike by [F2]; [F5] identifies this tangent Lorentz space with the standard Lorentz vector space, and [F6] makes its orthogonal complement positive definite. Thus the Hessian is positive on every nonzero $W\\perp_g V$, so its kernel is precisely $\\mathbb RV$ and its rank is three.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "The momentum in step 1.1 satisfies $g^{-1}(p,p)=(mc/s)^2g(V,V)=-m^2c^2$ and is future because it is a positive multiple of $V^\\flat$. Conversely, for any future covector on this shell, set $V=p^\\sharp$. Then $s=mc$, and the same formula returns $p$; every positive multiple of $V$ returns the same covector. This proves exact image and positive-ray fibres.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The defining function $C=\\tfrac12(g^{-1}(p,p)+m^2c^2)$ has fibre differential $d_pC=p^\\sharp\\ne0$ on the shell. By [F3] the shell is embedded. The Legendre map fixes the base point and has fibre differential of rank three by step 2.1, so its differential has rank $4+3=7$, the shell dimension; it is a submersion onto that image.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F3",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 2.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Euler's identity from [F4] gives $p(V)-L=0$, while the nonzero kernel direction in step 2.1 rules out a local diffeomorphism and any four-velocity inverse.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
