    "thm-rpm-h-regular-local-gauge-slice": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-rpm-h-first-class-local-reduction",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(P^{2n},\\omega)$ be symplectic, and let $N=\\{\\chi_1=\\cdots=\\chi_r=0\\}$ be a regular embedded level surface with $\\{\\chi_a,\\chi_b\\}=0$ on $N$. Then $\\ker(\\omega|_N)$ is exactly the rank-$r$ distribution spanned by the restricted Hamiltonian vector fields $X_{\\chi_a}$. This distribution is involutive. Around every point of $N$, its leaves admit smooth Frobenius coordinates $(s^1,\\ldots,s^r,y^1,\\ldots,y^{2n-2r})$, and the local leaf space has a unique smooth closed nondegenerate two-form $\\omega_{\\mathrm{red}}$ whose pullback is $\\omega|_N$. Thus regular first-class constraints yield a local symplectic quotient. No global orbit-space regularity follows from these hypotheses.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-rpm-h-second-class-dirac-bracket",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(P,\\omega)$ be symplectic and let $\\chi_1,\\ldots,\\chi_{2k}$ be smooth on an open set $U$, with $C_{ab}=\\{\\chi_a,\\chi_b\\}$ invertible at every point of $U$. Write $C^{ab}$ for its inverse and $N=\\{\\chi=0\\}\\cap U$. Then $N$ is an embedded symplectic submanifold, and the bracket\n\n$$\\{F,G\\}_D=\\{F,G\\}-\\{F,\\chi_a\\}C^{ab}\\{\\chi_b,G\\}$$\n\nis the Poisson bracket of the restricted symplectic form on $N$. It is independent of ambient extensions of $F,G$, and every $\\chi_a$ is a Casimir. This is local and assumes invertibility on $U$; it does not cover singular points.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-choice-free-smooth-inverse-function-theorem-in-euclidean-space",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In ZF, let $n\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be smooth, and let $a\\in U$. If $Df(a)$ is invertible, then there are open neighbourhoods $a\\in V\\subseteq U$ and $f(a)\\in W$ such that $f|_V:V\\to W$ is a diffeomorphism. Writing $g=(f|_V)^{-1}$,\n$$Dg(y)=Df(g(y))^{-1}\\qquad(y\\in W).$$\nNo choice axiom is used.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "On the common zero set, the combined matrix has block form $\\begin{pmatrix}B&A\\\\-A^T&D\\end{pmatrix}$, where $B=\\{\\chi,\\chi\\}=0$ there by first-classness and $D=\\{\\phi,\\phi\\}$. If it annihilates $(u,v)$, its first block equation is $Av=0$, so $v=0$; its second is $-A^Tu=0$, so $u=0$. Thus it is invertible; continuity preserves invertibility on a smaller neighborhood.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "[F2] applied to the combined list makes its zero surface embedded and symplectic with the displayed Dirac bracket.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "In Frobenius coordinates from [F1] around $z_0$, use the local frame $X_{\\chi_a}$ along each leaf. At $z_0$, its derivative on the gauge map has entries $X_{\\chi_a}(\\phi_b)=\\{\\phi_b,\\chi_a\\}=-A_{ab}$, so it is invertible; changing to the $s$-coordinate frame multiplies by an invertible matrix. For $r\\ge1$, [F3] therefore gives a local diffeomorphism of the gauge map along each leaf.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "At $z_0=(s_0,y_0)$ the gauge map has value $\\phi(z_0)=0$ by the statement. Consider the equal-dimensional map $(s,y)\\mapsto(\\phi_1(s,y),\\ldots,\\phi_r(s,y),y)$. Its derivative at $z_0$ is block triangular with invertible diagonal blocks by step 2.2 and the identity on $y$. Applying [F3] gives a neighborhood on which this map is a diffeomorphism; therefore, for each nearby $y$, the gauge equations have exactly one local solution $s$. This proves exactly one intersection within that product neighborhood, with no claim about distant portions of a leaf.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F3",
            "step 2.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
