    "thm-rpm-h-first-order-action-and-geodesics": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a smooth four-manifold without boundary, so that $M$ is Hausdorff\nand second countable and carries a maximal smooth atlas\n([[def-smooth-manifold]]). Let $g$ be a smooth covariant $2$-tensor field on $M$\nin the sense of [[def-gr-smooth-covariant-two-tensor-field]]. Then $g$ assigns\nto every $p\\in M$ a bilinear form $g_p:T_pM\\times T_pM\\to\\mathbb R$\n([[def-derivation-at-a-point-and-tangent-space]]).\n\n**(a) Lorentzian metric.** The field $g$ is a **Lorentzian metric of signature\n$(-,+,+,+)$** on $M$ when it is symmetric and nondegenerate, and at every\n$p\\in M$ the pair $(T_pM,g_p)$ is a Lorentz vector space of signature\n$(-,+,+,+)$ in the sense of [[def-lorentz-form-and-inertia]]: that is, $g_p$ has\ninertia $(3,1,0)$, so that some — equivalently every — basis of $T_pM$ in which\nthe matrix of $g_p$ is diagonal has exactly one negative and three positive\ndiagonal entries. The pair $(M,g)$ is a **smooth Lorentzian four-manifold** (or\nLorentzian manifold of signature $(-,+,+,+)$). In a smooth chart $(U,x)$ the\n**components** of $g$ are the $C^\\infty$ functions\n$g_{ab}(q)=g_q(\\partial_a|_q,\\partial_b|_q)$ of\n[[def-gr-smooth-covariant-two-tensor-field]], and $g_{ab}=g_{ba}$ by symmetry.\nWe use indices $a,b,c,d=0,1,2,3$ and the Einstein summation convention, with\ncomponents in a chart written as lower-case indexed symbols and equations\nbetween indexed fields understood basis-independently. These conventions are\nfixed once for this library and the physical reading of the symbols is recorded\nwith the framework postulates, separately from this mathematical definition.\n\n**(b) Pointwise causal structure.** For every $p\\in M$ the vocabulary of\n[[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]] applies to the Lorentz vector\nspace $(T_pM,g_p)$: a tangent vector $v\\in T_pM$ is **timelike** when\n$g_p(v,v)<0$, **null** when $v\\neq0$ and $g_p(v,v)=0$, **spacelike** when\n$g_p(v,v)>0$, and **causal** when it is timelike or null. Every nonzero\n$v\\in T_pM$ has exactly one causal character. The **timelike set**\n$T_p:=\\{v\\in T_pM:g_p(v,v)<0\\}$ has exactly two connected components, each\nnonempty, open, convex and path-connected; each $g_p$-orthonormal basis\n$e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3$ of $T_pM$ satisfies $g_p(e_a,e_b)=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)_{ab}$\nfor some ordering, so in particular nonzero orthonormal vectors exist at every\npoint ([[lem-lorentz-form-has-orthonormal-basis]],\n[[lem-timelike-cone-has-two-convex-components]]).\n\n**(c) Time orientation.** A **time orientation** of $(M,g)$, also called a\n**choice of future cone**, is a smooth vector field $N$ on $M$\n([[def-smooth-vector-field-as-a-tangent-bundle-section]], whose tangent-bundle\nsmooth structure uses the axiom $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ of\n[[def-countable-choice]]; this is the only use of that axiom in this\ndefinition) such that\n\n$$g_p(N_p,N_p)<0\\qquad\\text{for every }p\\in M .$$\n\nThe function $p\\mapsto g_p(N_p,N_p)$ is $C^\\infty$ because $g$ is a smooth\ncovariant $2$-tensor field and $N$ a smooth field, so the condition is a\npointwise strict inequality between a smooth function and $0$; since\n$g_p(N_p,N_p)<0$ implies $N_p\\neq0$, a time orientation is nowhere vanishing.\nA **time-oriented smooth Lorentzian four-manifold** is a triple $(M,g,N)$\nconsisting of a smooth Lorentzian four-manifold and a time orientation $N$.\n\nGiven a time orientation $N$ and $p\\in M$, the vector $N_p$ is timelike, and we\n**define** a nonzero causal vector $v\\in T_pM$ to be **future-directed** when\n$g_p(v,N_p)<0$ and **past-directed** when $g_p(v,N_p)>0$. The classification of\nnonzero causal vectors into exactly these two classes, and its agreement with\nthe two components of the timelike set (the future component is the component\n$C_p^+$ containing $N_p$, and the future causal vectors form\n$\\operatorname{cl}(C_p^+)\\setminus\\{0\\}$), is proved in\n[[lem-gr-future-causal-classification-by-a-timelike-field]]; in particular no\nnonzero causal $v$ satisfies $g_p(v,N_p)=0$, so the definition is exhaustive and\nmutually exclusive. Replacing $N$ by $-N$ reverses every future/past label, and\ntwo time orientations $N,N'$ determine the same future-directed causal vectors\nat every point where $g_p(N_p,N'_p)<0$, while they determine opposite ones\nwhere $g_p(N_p,N'_p)>0$, by\n[[lem-gr-timelike-fields-define-equal-or-opposite-future-cones]]; that lemma\nalso shows the sign of $p\\mapsto g_p(N_p,N'_p)$ is constant on every connected\ncomponent of $M$.\n\n**(d) The inverse metric.** Because $g_p$ is nondegenerate, it induces at each\n$p$ an isomorphism $\\flat_p:T_pM\\to T_p^*M$, $v\\mapsto g_p(v,\\cdot)$, and an\ninverse metric $g_p^{-1}$ on the dual space; the inverse metric, the musical\nisomorphisms and the components $g^{ab}$ are defined separately on this page,\nwhere the smoothness of those components is also proved. Neither construction\nuses any choice principle beyond the one already recorded in (c).\n\n\n**(e) Smooth timelike curves and their scalar normalization.** Before using\nthese quantities in the physical conventions, fix a positive real scaling\nparameter $c$ (with no physical meaning or dimension in this mathematical\nitem). A **smooth future timelike curve** is a smooth map $\\gamma:I\\to M$\nfrom a nonempty open real interval $I$ ([[def-interval]],\n[[def-c-r-and-smooth-maps-between-smooth-manifolds]]) whose velocity derivation\n$\\gamma'(s)\\in T_{\\gamma(s)}M$\n([[def-velocity-derivation-of-a-smooth-curve]]) satisfies\n$g(\\gamma',\\gamma')<0$ and $g(\\gamma',N)<0$ at every $s\\in I$.\nIn a chart its components are $d(x^a\\circ\\gamma)/ds$, $a=0,1,2,3$.\nFor a supplied base point $s_0\\in I$ and additive constant $\\tau_0\\in\\mathbb R$\ndefine\n\n$$h(s):=\\sqrt{-g_{\\gamma(s)}(\\gamma'(s),\\gamma'(s))},\\qquad \\tau(s):=\\tau_0+\\frac1c\\int_{s_0}^{s}h(r)\\,dr,\\qquad U(s):=\\frac{c\\gamma'(s)}{h(s)}.$$\n\nThe integral is oriented when $s<s_0$. The function $\\tau$ is called\n**proper time** for this scaling and $U$ the **four-velocity** of the smooth\ncurve; its increment times $c$ is the Lorentzian length. The well-definedness\nproof below shows $\\tau'>0$, that $\\tau:I\\to\\tau(I)$ is a smooth\ndiffeomorphism, and that $U$ is the velocity of $\\gamma\\circ\\tau^{-1}$.\nThus $g(U,U)=-c^2$ and $U$ is future timelike. This introduces the smooth\ncase before any physical unit reading; the later worldline definition extends\nit to its stated $C^k$, endpoint and piecewise-$C^1$ classes. It asserts no\nclock coupling, motion law or physical value of $c$.\n\n**Convention and scope.** The physical reading of a smooth time-oriented\nLorentzian four-manifold as a classical spacetime model, and the domain in which\nthat reading is adopted, are recorded in the framework postulate\n`post-gr-spacetime-and-classical-domain`; the words spacetime and event carry no\nmathematical content beyond this definition, and this item asserts no claim\nabout nature. The dimension $4$ and the absence of boundary are part of the\ndata; nothing in the definition assumes that $M$ is nonempty or connected, and\nno completeness, extendibility or maximality property of $(M,g)$ is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a smooth four-manifold without boundary and let $g$ be a smooth\nsymmetric covariant $2$-tensor field on $M$ such that $g_p$ is nondegenerate at\nevery $p\\in M$ ([[def-gr-smooth-covariant-two-tensor-field]]). Assume\n$\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ for the canonical tangent-bundle smooth structure\n([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-smooth-vector-field-as-a-tangent-bundle-section]]).\nFor this general metric define $\\flat_p(v):=g_p(v,\\cdot)$,\n$\\sharp_p:=\\flat_p^{-1}$, and\n$g_p^{-1}(\\alpha,\\beta):=g_p(\\sharp_p\\alpha,\\sharp_p\\beta)$.\nNondegeneracy makes $\\flat_p$ injective and hence invertible between the\nfour-dimensional tangent space and its dual. In a chart, $g^{ab}$ are the\nentries of the inverse matrix of $(g_{ab})$. These definitions agree with\n[[def-gr-inverse-metric-and-musical-isomorphisms]] in its Lorentzian scope.\nHere and below repeated upper/lower indices are summed over $0,1,2,3$.\nThis holds in particular\nwhen $(M,g,N)$ is a time-oriented smooth Lorentzian four-manifold and $g$ is\nits metric ([[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]). For smooth local vector fields\n$X,Y,Z$ put\n\n$$K(X,Y,Z):=X\\bigl(g(Y,Z)\\bigr)+Y\\bigl(g(Z,X)\\bigr)-Z\\bigl(g(X,Y)\\bigr)+g([X,Y],Z)+g([Z,X],Y)-g([Y,Z],X),$$\n\nwith brackets as in [[def-lie-bracket-of-smooth-vector-fields]].\n\n**(a) Existence and formulas.** There is exactly one affine connection\n$\\nabla$ on $M$ ([[def-affine-connection-on-a-smooth-manifold]]) satisfying\n\n$$2g(\\nabla_XY,Z)=K(X,Y,Z)\\qquad\\text{for all smooth local }X,Y,Z .$$\n\nIt is torsion-free and $g$-compatible, so it is a metric-selected connection\nfor $(M,g)$, meaning torsion-free and satisfying\n$Xg(Y,Z)=g(\\nabla_XY,Z)+g(Y,\\nabla_XZ)$ for smooth local fields\n(the conditions of [[def-gr-metric-selected-connection]] extended here to\nnondegenerate $g$); in every smooth chart its\nChristoffel symbols are\n\n$$\\Gamma^a{}_{bc}=\\tfrac12\\,g^{ad}\\bigl(\\partial_bg_{dc}+\\partial_cg_{bd}-\\partial_dg_{bc}\\bigr),$$\n\nwith $g^{ad}$ the inverse-metric components defined above\n([[def-christoffel-symbols-of-an-affine-connection]]).\n\n**(b) Uniqueness.** If an affine connection $\\nabla'$ on $M$ is torsion-free\nand $g$-compatible, then $\\nabla'=\\nabla$. Consequently the class\n$\\operatorname{LC}(M,g)$ of metric-selected connections has exactly one\nelement; it is the **Levi–Civita connection** of $g$.\n\n**(c) Induced derivative on tensor fields.** Let $\\nabla^*$ be the dual\nconnection on $T^*M$ and let the product connection of\n[[def-product-connection-on-tensor-and-hom-bundles]] act on\n$T^r_sM:=(TM)^{\\otimes r}\\otimes(T^*M)^{\\otimes s}$, for integers $r,s\\ge0$,\nconstructed by [[lem-finite-tensor-products-of-smooth-vector-bundles]]. A\ntype $(r,s)$ tensor at $p$ is a multilinear map\n$(T_p^*M)^r\\times(T_pM)^s\\to\\mathbb R$\n([[def-type-r-s-tensor-on-a-finite-dimensional-vector-space]]); a smooth\ntensor field is a section with smooth coefficients in each local product\nframe. The empty product is the scalar line. Its chart components are\n$T^{a_1\\cdots a_r}{}_{b_1\\cdots b_s}=T(dx^{a_1},\\ldots,dx^{a_r},\\partial_{b_1},\\ldots,\\partial_{b_s})$,\nwhere $dx^a$ is dual to $\\partial_a$. This agrees with\n[[def-gr-tensor-fields-and-components]] in the Lorentzian case. Put\n$T^{a_1\\cdots a_r}{}_{b_1\\cdots b_s;c}:=(\\nabla_{\\partial_c}T)^{a_1\\cdots a_r}{}_{b_1\\cdots b_s}$.\nThen, in every smooth chart, for\nevery smooth tensor field $T$ of type $(r,s)$,\n\n$$T^{a_1\\cdots a_r}{}_{b_1\\cdots b_s;c}=\\partial_cT^{a_1\\cdots a_r}{}_{b_1\\cdots b_s}+\\sum_{i=1}^{r}\\Gamma^{a_i}{}_{dc}\\,T^{a_1\\cdots d\\cdots a_r}{}_{b_1\\cdots b_s}-\\sum_{j=1}^{s}\\Gamma^{d}{}_{b_jc}\\,T^{a_1\\cdots a_r}{}_{b_1\\cdots d\\cdots b_s},$$\n\nwhere the slot marked $d$ replaces the named index; this derivative commutes\nwith every contraction in the sense of [[def-contraction-of-a-mixed-tensor]],\nand it annihilates the metric and the inverse metric:\n\n$$g_{ab;c}=0,\\qquad g^{ab}{}_{;c}=0 .$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-geodesic-of-an-affine-connection",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a smooth manifold without boundary with affine connection $\\nabla$, and let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval with nonempty interior. A smooth curve $\\gamma:I\\to M$ is an **affinely parametrized geodesic** when\n$$D_t\\gamma'(t)=0\\qquad(t\\in I),$$\nwith one-sided interpretation at an included endpoint. Constant curves are geodesics. Unless another parametrization is explicitly stated, “geodesic” means affinely parametrized geodesic.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2",
            "3.2",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-covariant-derivative-along-a-curve",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a given connection on $E$ and a smooth curve on an interval with nonempty interior, the **covariant derivative along the curve** is\n$$D_tV=(\\gamma^*\\nabla)_{\\partial/\\partial t}V.$$\nHere $V$ is a section of the pullback bundle as in [[def-vector-field-and-section-along-a-smooth-curve]], and the pullback connection exists by [[thm-pullback-connection-is-well-defined-and-functorial]]. It is real-linear and satisfies $D_t(fV)=f'V+fD_tV$ for functions of the parameter.\n\nAt an included endpoint the smooth up-to-boundary coefficients have their one-sided derivative; equivalently any smooth local extension of the coefficients gives this value, because extensions agreeing on a one-sided interval have the same derivative there. For a piecewise smooth curve the derivative is defined on each piece, with separate one-sided derivatives at corners; those derivatives are not required to agree. A singleton interval carries no derivative operator under this convention and will have identity transport by definition. A zero section or a rank-zero bundle gives zero covariant derivative. Constant base curves can have sections with nonzero coefficient derivative.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-cm-stationarity-iff-euler-lagrange",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(O,\\mathcal L)$ be a time-dependent Lagrangian chart over $[a,b]\\times U$ in the sense of\n[[def-cm-time-dependent-lagrangian-and-action]] and let $q:[a,b]\\to U$ be a $C^2$ admissible curve.\nThen $q$ is stationary for the action $S$ if and only if\n\n$$E_{\\mathcal L}(q)(t)=0\\qquad\\text{for every }t\\in[a,b].$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-rpm-h-regular-constraint-system",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_{\\omega}$ and let $(M^{2n},\\omega)$ be a smooth symplectic manifold. A finite family of smooth functions $\\chi_1,\\ldots,\\chi_r$ defined on an open neighborhood $U\\subseteq M$ is a **regular primary constraint family** on\n\n$$C:=\\{z\\in U:\\chi_1(z)=\\cdots=\\chi_r(z)=0\\}$$\n\nwhen $d\\chi_1(z),\\ldots,d\\chi_r(z)$ are linearly independent at every $z\\in C$. The regular-level theorem [[thm-a-regular-level-set-is-an-embedded-submanifold]] then gives $C$ its embedded-submanifold structure and $T_zC=\\bigcap_a\\ker d\\chi_a(z)$. For functions $F,G$ on $U$, write $F\\approx G$ when $F|_C=G|_C$; thus $F\\approx0$ means that $F$ vanishes on $C$, without requiring it to vanish off $C$.\n\nGiven a smooth canonical Hamiltonian $H_c$ on $U$ and multiplier functions $u^a(t)$, the **total generator** is\n\n$$H_T(t,z):=H_c(t,z)+\\sum_{a=1}^r u^a(t)\\chi_a(z).$$\n\nThe functions $u^a$ are independent parameter functions; the definition does not assert that their equations determine them. If $H_c$ is an energy and $\\chi_a$ has units $[\\chi_a]$, then $u^a$ has units energy divided by $[\\chi_a]$, so every term in $H_T$ has the same units. A later constraint, if generated by consistency, is not declared primary by this definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-rpm-h-canonical-poisson-and-flow",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $Q$ be a finite-dimensional smooth manifold as in [[def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space]], and write its cotangent phase space in induced coordinates $(q^i,p_i)$. With $\\omega=dq^i\\wedge dp_i$, define $X_H$ by $\\iota_{X_H}\\omega=dH$ and define the Poisson bracket by $\\{F,G\\}:=X_G(F)$. Then\n\n$$X_H=\\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial p_i}\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial q^i}-\\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial q^i}\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial p_i},\\qquad \\{F,G\\}=F_{q^i}G_{p_i}-F_{p_i}G_{q^i}.$$\n\nThe bracket is coordinate independent and makes smooth functions a Poisson algebra. If $H_t$ is smooth on an open time-dependent domain $D\\subset\\mathbb R\\times T^*Q$, each solution of $\\dot z=X_{H_t}(z)$ has local uniqueness and smooth dependence on initial data and parameters; on its actual interval,\n\n$$\\frac{d}{dt}F(t,z(t))=\\partial_tF+\\{F,H_t\\}.$$\n\nNo completeness or global flow is asserted. If $H$ is smooth, each local time-$t$ flow map preserves ω.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-rpm-h-homogeneous-massive-shell",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g,T)$ be a smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold. At $x\\in M$, for $p\\in T_x^*M$, define $p^\\sharp$ as the unique vector satisfying $g_x(p^\\sharp,W)=p(W)$ for every $W\\in T_xM$, and set $g_x^{-1}(p,r):=g_x(p^\\sharp,r^\\sharp)$. In a chart, $p^\\sharp=g^{ab}p_b\\partial_a$, where $(g^{ab})$ is the inverse matrix of $g_{ab}=g(\\partial_a,\\partial_b)$. A covector on this background is future when its metric dual is future-directed. Let $m,c>0$ and $L(x,V)=-mc\\sqrt{-g(V,V)}$ on the future timelike tangent cone. Its fibre derivative is\n\n$$p_a=mc\\frac{g_{ab}V^b}{s},\\qquad s=\\sqrt{-g(V,V)}>0.$$\n\nThe velocity Hessian is $mc(g_{ab}/s+V_aV_b/s^3)$, has kernel exactly $\\mathbb RV$, and rank three. The image consists exactly of the future shell $\\Sigma_m^+=\\{(x,p)\\in T^*M:g_x^{-1}(p,p)=-m^2c^2,\\ p^\\sharp\\text{ future}\\}$. Each fibre is a connected positive ray, and the Legendre map is a surjective submersion onto this embedded image. Its energy $p(V)-L$ is zero; the map has no local inverse in the four-velocity direction.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Use the supplied smooth tangent and cotangent bundles under [A1], with the mathematical functionals and selected branches [F1,F2]. In a spacetime chart, variation in $e$ gives $C_m=0$. Variation in $p_a$ gives $x'^a=e g^{ab}p_b$. Variation in $x^a$, integrating the $p_a\\delta x'^a$ term by parts with $\\delta x$ vanishing at endpoints, gives $p'_a=-e\\partial_a C_m=-\\tfrac e2\\partial_a g^{bc}p_bp_c$. The vertical differential of $C_m$ is $d_pC_m=p^\\sharp$; it is nonzero on the massive branch because $g^{-1}(p,p)=-m^2c^2<0$, and on the massless branch because $p\\ne0$ and $g^{-1}$ is nondegenerate. Thus the shell is a regular primary constraint by [F7], and [F8] fixes the canonical sign convention. The equations are tensorial and hence agree across overlapping charts.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For $m>0$, the first equation gives $p_a=g_{ab}x'^b/e$. The constraint gives $g(x',x')=-e^2m^2c^2$, so $s=\\sqrt{-g(x',x')}=emc$ because $e,m,c>0$. Hence $p_a=mc\\,g_{ab}x'^b/s$, the fibre derivative of the square-root Lagrangian; this agrees with the exact image result [F9].",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F9",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "Differentiate $p_a=g_{ab}x'^b/e$, use the second equation, and use the inverse-metric identity $\\partial_a g^{bc}g_{bi}g_{cj}=-\\partial_a g_{ij}$. Multiplying by $e$ gives $$g_{ab}x''^b+(\\partial_cg_{ab})x'^cx'^b-\\frac{e'}e g_{ab}x'^b=\\frac12(\\partial_ag_{ij})x'^ix'^j.$$ Since $x'^cx'^b$ is symmetric, the difference of the metric-derivative terms is $\\frac12(\\partial_cg_{ab}+\\partial_bg_{ac}-\\partial_ag_{bc})x'^cx'^b=g_{ad}\\Gamma^d{}_{cb}x'^cx'^b$. Inverting $g_{ab}$ therefore gives $$\\nabla_{x'}x'=\\frac{e'}e x'.$$ This computation uses [F3]–[F5]; it is the coordinate form of the reparameterized geodesic equation.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Substituting $p_a=g_{ab}x'^b/e$ into the integrand gives $L_1=g(x',x')/(2e)-em^2c^2/2=-s^2/(2e)-em^2c^2/2$, where $s=\\sqrt{-g(x',x')}>0$. Its $e$-derivative is $s^2/(2e^2)-m^2c^2/2$, whose unique positive zero is $e=s/(mc)$; the second derivative there is $-s^2/e^3\\ne0$. Thus the $p$- and $e$-stationarity equations determine a unique smooth auxiliary pair $(p^*(x,x'),e^*(x,x'))$ on the future timelike domain, and the stationary integrand is $-mc\\,s$. For any fixed-endpoint variation $x_\\varepsilon$, choose $p_\\varepsilon,e_\\varepsilon$ by these formulas. The chain rule for the varied action has the $x_\\varepsilon$ variation plus terms proportional to its $p$- and $e$-derivatives; those terms vanish at the auxiliary stationary pair. Hence the first variation of the reduced action equals the $x$-part of the first-order variation. Conversely every stationary first-order triple must equal this unique auxiliary pair, so the same identity gives the reverse implication. This proves the equivalence with the square-root action.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-2",
          "claim": "For $m>0$, $d\\tau=s\\,d\\lambda/c=em\\,d\\lambda$, so $e=1/m$ in proper-time parameter and step 2.2 becomes $\\nabla_UU=0$; the converse follows by reversing the momentum definition and the same identities.",
          "step": "3.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "For $m=0$, [F2] selects $p\\ne0$ future null; the first equation makes $x'=e p^\\sharp$, so $x'$ is nonzero future null and the constraint is null. Choose a new parameter σ with $d\\sigma/d\\lambda=e/e_0$ for any constant $e_0>0$; the connection transformation law applied to step 2.2 gives $\\nabla_{dx/d\\sigma}(dx/d\\sigma)=0$. Since $g(x',x')=0$, the massive proper-time construction cannot be used.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
