    "thm-rpm-h-inertial-time-reduced-hamiltonian": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice $\\mathrm{AC}_{\\omega}$: every sequence of nonempty sets has a choice function ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $Q$ be a finite-dimensional, Hausdorff, second-countable smooth manifold without boundary, of dimension $n$. Its cotangent bundle is\n\n$$T^*Q=\\coprod_{q\\in Q}T_q^*Q,\\qquad \\pi:T^*Q\\to Q,\\quad \\pi(q,p)=q,$$\n\nwith the canonical smooth structure and induced cotangent charts supplied by the manifold construction in [[def-tautological-one-form-on-a-cotangent-bundle]]. The **tautological one-form** is $\\theta_{(q,p)}(\\xi)=p(d\\pi_{(q,p)}\\xi)$, and the **canonical cotangent two-form** is $\\omega=-d\\theta$. The exact theorem [[thm-the-canonical-cotangent-two-form-is-symplectic]] makes $(T^*Q,\\omega)$ a symplectic manifold in the sense of [[def-symplectic-form-and-symplectic-manifold]]. We call this pair the **cotangent phase space** of $Q$.\n\nIn an induced chart $(q^1,\\ldots,q^n,p_1,\\ldots,p_n)$, $\\theta=p_i\\,dq^i$ and $\\omega=dq^i\\wedge dp_i$. A mathematical **observable** on this phase space means a smooth function $F:T^*Q\\to\\mathbb R$; this definition does not assert that a measurement has been performed or that every such function is physically measurable. For $G\\in C^\\infty(T^*Q)$, let $X_G$ be the unique vector field satisfying $\\iota_{X_G}\\omega=dG$, which exists because $\\omega$ is nondegenerate, and define the canonical bracket by $\\{F,G\\}:=dF(X_G)$. In the induced chart, contraction gives $X_G=G_{p_i}\\partial_{q^i}-G_{q^i}\\partial_{p_i}$, so\n\n$$\\{F,G\\}=F_{q^i}G_{p_i}-F_{p_i}G_{q^i}.$$\n\nFor an interpreted particle model, assign coordinates $q^i$ their declared units and assign the conjugate covector components $p_i$ units of action divided by the units of $q^i$. Thus $\\theta$ and $\\omega$ have action units, conventionally joule-seconds, and the defined canonical bracket of quantities with units $[F]$ and $[G]$ has units $[F][G]/(\\text{action})$. These are dimensional interpretations of the model, not additional mathematical premises. The use of $\\mathrm{AC}_{\\omega}$ is exactly the use in the smooth cotangent-bundle suppliers; no full Axiom of Choice is used.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "An **oriented time-oriented affine Minkowski space** is a quadruple\n$(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ with the following data. In this notation $V$ includes its\nspecified orientation; forgetting that orientation gives only the underlying\nreal vector space, not the full datum $V$ of the model.\n\n1. A nonempty affine space $A$ over a $4$-dimensional real vector space $V$,\n   with action $(p,v)\\mapsto p+v$ and displacement $q-p\\in V$\n   ([[def-affine-space-and-displacement]]). In particular $V$ is a real vector\n   space of dimension $4$.\n2. A Lorentz form $\\eta$ on $V$, i.e. a nondegenerate symmetric bilinear form of\n   inertia $(3,1,0)$ ([[def-lorentz-form-and-inertia]]). Its associated quadratic\n   form is $q(v)=\\eta(v,v)$.\n3. An **orientation** of $V$: a positive ray of the determinant line\n   $\\Lambda^4V$, fixed once and for all\n   ([[def-determinant-line-orientation-of-a-finite-dimensional-real-vector-space]]).\n4. A **time orientation** $C^+$ of $(V,\\eta)$: one of the two connected\n   components of the timelike set $T=\\{v\\in V:\\eta(v,v)<0\\}$, the other being\n   $C^-:=T\\setminus C^+$ ([[def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets]]).\n\nThe following terminology is attached to such a quadruple.\n\n- The elements $p$ of $A$ are the **events**; for events $p,q$ the vector\n  $q-p\\in V$ is the **displacement from $p$ to $q$**.\n- The **interval** of $p,q\\in A$ is the real number\n  $I(p,q):=\\eta(q-p,q-p)=q(q-p)$.\n- Events $p,q$ are **timelike**, **null**, **spacelike** or **causally\n  related** when the displacement $q-p$ is timelike, null, spacelike or causal\n  in the sense of [[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]]; they are\n  **future-related** when $q-p$ is future causal and **past-related** when\n  $q-p$ is past causal in the sense of\n  [[def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets]].\n\n**No point of $A$ is distinguished.** For every $v\\in V$ the translation\n$\\tau_v:A\\to A$, $\\tau_v(p):=p+v$, is a bijection, since it is inverse to\n$\\tau_{-v}$: $\\tau_{-v}(\\tau_v(p))=(p+v)+(-v)=p+(v-v)=p$ and symmetrically, by\nthe affine action axioms ([[def-affine-space-and-displacement]]). Displacements\nare translation invariant, $\\tau_v(q)-\\tau_v(p)=q-p$, so every $\\tau_v$ preserves\nthe interval, the causal classification of every ordered pair, and the sets\n$C^+$, $C^-$ (which are subsets of $V$, independent of the point of $A$); it\nchanges only the labelling of points. The mathematical structure of the model is\nexactly the quadruple $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$; no derived physical quantity (proper\ntime, four-velocity, momentum, energy, charge or any field) is part of this\ndefinition, and all later structure of the framework (frames, charts, tensor\nfields, worldlines) is built on this model.\n\n**Well-definedness.** The affine-space definition supplies the action and the\nunique displacements; the Lorentz-form definition supplies $\\eta$ and its\ninertia; the determinant-line definition supplies orientations; and the two\ncomponents of $T$ between which the time-orientation definition chooses exist by\nthe components lemma recorded in the well-definedness clause of\n[[def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets]], which is also what makes the\nnames future and past causal in item 4 well defined.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-rpm-h-flat-shell-global-reduction",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In affine Minkowski space with inertial coordinates $x^0,x^i$ measured in metres and metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, identify each cotangent vector $p$ with its metric dual $p^\\sharp\\in V$, the unique vector satisfying $\\eta(p^\\sharp,v)=p(v)$ for all $v\\in V$. In these coordinates $p^\\sharp=(-p_0,p_1,p_2,p_3)$. Let $m\\ge0$ and let\n\n$$\\Sigma_m^+=\\{(x,p): -p_0=P^0=\\sqrt{m^2c^2+|\\mathbf p|^2},\\ p_0<0\\},$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf p\\ne0$ when $m=0$, and write $P^i=p_i$ for the spatial raised momentum components. The characteristic flow of the restricted canonical form is $(x,p)\\mapsto(x+\\alpha P,p)$, and its orbit space is globally diffeomorphic to $B_m=\\mathbb R^3_a\\times\\mathbb R^3_p$ for $m>0$, or $\\mathbb R^3_a\\times(\\mathbb R^3_p\\setminus\\{0\\})$ for $m=0$, by\n\n$$a^i=x^i-\\frac{P^i}{P^0}x^0,\\qquad p_i=p_i.$$\n\nThe reduced symplectic form is $da^i\\wedge dp_i$. The quotient is Hausdorff and second countable and each full characteristic line meets each slice $x^0=ct$ exactly once. This is an explicit free flat quotient, not a conclusion about arbitrary first-class systems.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-rpm-h-first-order-action-and-geodesics",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g,T)$ be a prescribed smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold with signature $(-+++)$. Work in a spacetime chart on a compact parameter interval $[a,b]$ with $a<b$; take $x,p,e$ smooth on a neighbourhood of that interval, $e>0$, $m\\ge0$, and $c>0$. Select stationary triples on the future shell branch after taking the independent variations: when $m>0$, $p^\\sharp$ is future timelike; when $m=0$, require $p\\ne0$ and $p^\\sharp$ future null. Set $C_m=\\tfrac12(g^{ab}p_ap_b+m^2c^2)$ and define the first-order functional\n\n$$I_m[x,p,e]=\\int_a^b\\left(p_ax'^a-eC_m(x,p)\\right)d\\lambda.$$\n\nTake independent smooth variations of $p,e$ and fixed-endpoint variations of $x$. Stationarity on the chart segment is equivalent to\n\n$$x'^a=e g^{ab}p_b,\\quad p'_a=-\\tfrac e2\\partial_a g^{bc}p_bp_c,\\quad C_m=0.$$\n\nOn the massive future branch $m>0$, these equations are equivalent to stationary paths of the mathematical square-root functional $S[x]=-mc\\int_a^b\\sqrt{-g(x\\prime,x\\prime)}\\,d\\lambda$ on future-timelike curves with fixed endpoints; the fibre-derivative covector is $p=mc\\,x'^\\flat/\\sqrt{-g(x',x')}$, and proper-time gauge has $e=1/m$. For the separate nonzero future-null branch $m=0$, the equations describe a null geodesic with an arbitrary positive parameter multiplier; no proper-time parameter exists. In both cases the unparameterized path is a geodesic, and no existence or completeness claim is made.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-rpm-h-second-class-dirac-bracket",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(P,\\omega)$ be symplectic and let $\\chi_1,\\ldots,\\chi_{2k}$ be smooth on an open set $U$, with $C_{ab}=\\{\\chi_a,\\chi_b\\}$ invertible at every point of $U$. Write $C^{ab}$ for its inverse and $N=\\{\\chi=0\\}\\cap U$. Then $N$ is an embedded symplectic submanifold, and the bracket\n\n$$\\{F,G\\}_D=\\{F,G\\}-\\{F,\\chi_a\\}C^{ab}\\{\\chi_b,G\\}$$\n\nis the Poisson bracket of the restricted symplectic form on $N$. It is independent of ambient extensions of $F,G$, and every $\\chi_a$ is a Casimir. This is local and assumes invertibility on $U$; it does not cover singular points.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-rpm-h-canonical-poisson-and-flow",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $Q$ be a finite-dimensional smooth manifold as in [[def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space]], and write its cotangent phase space in induced coordinates $(q^i,p_i)$. With $\\omega=dq^i\\wedge dp_i$, define $X_H$ by $\\iota_{X_H}\\omega=dH$ and define the Poisson bracket by $\\{F,G\\}:=X_G(F)$. Then\n\n$$X_H=\\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial p_i}\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial q^i}-\\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial q^i}\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial p_i},\\qquad \\{F,G\\}=F_{q^i}G_{p_i}-F_{p_i}G_{q^i}.$$\n\nThe bracket is coordinate independent and makes smooth functions a Poisson algebra. If $H_t$ is smooth on an open time-dependent domain $D\\subset\\mathbb R\\times T^*Q$, each solution of $\\dot z=X_{H_t}(z)$ has local uniqueness and smooth dependence on initial data and parameters; on its actual interval,\n\n$$\\frac{d}{dt}F(t,z(t))=\\partial_tF+\\{F,H_t\\}.$$\n\nNo completeness or global flow is asserted. If $H$ is smooth, each local time-$t$ flow map preserves ω.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The shell equation is $-p_0^2+|\\mathbf p|^2=-m^2c^2$. Its future branch has $p_0=-\\sqrt{m^2c^2+|\\mathbf p|^2}$. Multiplying the positive root by $c$ gives $E=\\sqrt{m^2c^4+c^2|\\mathbf p|^2}$, so $p_0=-E/c$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "On the gauge $x^0=ct$, the canonical term is $p_0c+p_i\\dot q^i=-E+p_i\\dot q^i$. The constraint term vanishes after solving $C_m=0$, yielding the displayed reduced action.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "Set $\\psi=x^0-ct$. From $C_m=\\tfrac12(-p_0^2+|\\mathbf p|^2+m^2c^2)$ and the canonical bracket, $\\{C_m,\\psi\\}=p_0=-P^0\\ne0$. Thus the matrix for $(C_m,\\psi)$ is invertible; [F5] proves the gauge slice is symplectic, and the inverse $2\\times2$ matrix gives $\\{q^i,p_j\\}_D=\\delta^i_j$ and zero spatial-spatial and momentum-momentum brackets.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Varying the reduced action with fixed spatial endpoints gives Hamilton equations $\\dot q^i=\\partial E/\\partial p_i=c^2p_i/E$ and $\\dot p_i=-\\partial E/\\partial q^i=0$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "In the unreduced equations (x'^i=e p_i, x'^0=e(-p_0)=eE/c); hence (dx^i/dt=x'^i/(dt/d\\lambda)=c^2p_i/E), while $p_i$ is constant. Because $E/c>0$, $t$ is strictly increasing and every complete affine characteristic line meets each fixed $t$ once, also as established by [F3].",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Statement — Free shell in inertial time has the square-root Hamiltonian: The conclusion concerns supplied local or finite data rather than an aggregate construction over an empty index set. The flat future root has positive energy for massive or nonzero null momentum. The inertial gauge pair is second class and its compact-interval reduction gives canonical spatial dynamics; only the full flat characteristic lines have global time-slice intersections."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement — Free shell in inertial time has the square-root Hamiltonian: The permitted zero values and nonzero normalization domain are fixed by the declared input conditions. The flat future root has positive energy for massive or nonzero null momentum. The inertial gauge pair is second class and its compact-interval reduction gives canonical spatial dynamics; only the full flat characteristic lines have global time-slice intersections."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
