    "pthm-rpm-h-action-mechanical-momentum": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-gr-timelike-worldline-and-proper-time",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be a time-oriented smooth Lorentzian four-manifold\n([[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]) and let $c>0$ be a fixed real constant.\nAll curves below take values in $M$; the physical reading of $c$, of a\nworldline as the history of a clock or body, and of the parameter values as\ntimes is not part of this definition and is adopted separately in the\npostulates of the framework.\n\n**(a) Regularity classes.** Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval with\nnonempty interior ([[def-interval]]) and let $\\gamma:I\\to M$ be a map. A\n**chart representative** of $\\gamma$ is the map $x\\circ\\gamma$ for a smooth\nchart $(U,x)$ of $M$ ([[def-smooth-manifold]]).\n\n- For $k\\in\\mathbb N_0\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$ and an interior point $t_0\\in I$, the map\n  $\\gamma$ is **$C^k$ at $t_0$** when there is a chart representative\n  $x\\circ\\gamma$ defined near $t_0$ that is $C^k$ there in the componentwise\n  Euclidean sense. This condition is independent of the chart: on the overlap\n  of two chart representatives the transition map is smooth and the chain rule\n  applies ([[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]],\n  [[def-c-r-and-smooth-maps-between-smooth-manifolds]]). If $t_0\\in I$ is an\n  included endpoint of $I$, the coordinate representative and its derivatives through order $k$\n  are continuous up to $t_0$ on the relative interval\n  $I\\cap(t_0-\\delta,t_0+\\delta)$ for some $\\delta>0$, with derivatives at\n  $t_0$ taken one-sided. For $k=\\infty$ this holds at every finite order.\n  Merely possessing a one-sided secant tangent, as defined next, does not\n  by itself make a curve $C^1$ at the endpoint. The map $\\gamma$ is **$C^k$ on $I$** when it is $C^k$ at\n  every point of $I$.\n\n- Let $b\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\varepsilon>0$. A curve\n  $\\gamma:(b-\\varepsilon,b]\\to M$ has **left tangent** $v\\in T_{\\gamma(b)}M$\n  at $b$ when for one — equivalently every — chart $(U,x)$ around\n  $\\gamma(b)$, writing $v=\\sum_{a=0}^{3}v^a\\partial_a|_{\\gamma(b)}$\n  ([[thm-coordinate-derivations-form-a-basis-of-the-tangent-space]]), the\n  difference quotients satisfy\n  $\\bigl(x^a(\\gamma(t))-x^a(\\gamma(b))\\bigr)/(t-b)\\to v^a$ as $t\\uparrow b$ for\n  $a=0,1,2,3$. A **right tangent** at the left end of a curve is defined\n  analogously with $t\\downarrow b$. Chart independence is the one-sided\n  chain-rule computation for the smooth transition maps\n  ([[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]]).\n\n- A curve $\\gamma:[a,b]\\to M$ with $a<b$ is **piecewise $C^1$** when there is a\n  finite partition $a=s_0<s_1<\\cdots<s_m=b$ such that for every $i$ the\n  restriction of $\\gamma$ to $[s_{i-1},s_i]$ is $C^1$ up to its included\n  endpoints in the one-sided sense above: in every endpoint chart its\n  component derivatives on the open piece have continuous one-sided limits,\n  agreeing with its endpoint derivatives. Equivalently each piece has a\n  $C^1$ extension to its closed parameter piece. In particular its right\n  tangent at $s_{i-1}$ and left tangent at $s_i$ exist. Such a\n  partition is called **admissible** for $\\gamma$. The piece extensions give continuity at the\n  corners; no agreement of the\n  two one-sided tangents at a corner is required.\n\n**(b) Velocity.** If $\\gamma$ is $C^1$ at an interior point $t$, its\n**velocity** $\\gamma'(t)\\in T_{\\gamma(t)}M$ is the derivation\n$[f]\\mapsto(f\\circ\\gamma)'(t)$ on smooth germs at $\\gamma(t)$, extending\n[[def-velocity-derivation-of-a-smooth-curve]] from smooth to $C^1$ curves.\nEqual germ representatives agree along the curve near $t$, and the\none-variable product rule proves the derivation identity. In a chart around\n$\\gamma(t)$ the velocity has the component\nexpression\n\n$$\\gamma'(t)=\\sum_{a=0}^{3}\\frac{d}{dt}\\bigl(x^a\\circ\\gamma\\bigr)(t)\\,\\partial_a|_{\\gamma(t)},$$\n\nthe coordinate expression of the derivation\n([[thm-coordinate-derivations-form-a-basis-of-the-tangent-space]]). At an\nincluded endpoint of the domain, $\\gamma'$ means the corresponding one-sided\ntangent of clause (a).\n\n**(c) Timelike worldlines.** For $k\\in\\{1,2,3,\\ldots\\}\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$,\na **$C^k$ future timelike worldline** is a $C^k$\ncurve $\\gamma:I\\to M$ whose velocity $\\gamma'(t)$ is future timelike at every\n$t\\in I$, in the sense of [[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]]\nand [[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]: $g_{\\gamma(t)}(\\gamma'(t),\\gamma'(t))<0$\nand $g_{\\gamma(t)}(\\gamma'(t),N_{\\gamma(t)})<0$. A **piecewise-$C^1$ future\ntimelike worldline** is a piecewise-$C^1$ curve $\\gamma:[a,b]\\to M$ whose\nvelocity at every point of each open piece and whose one-sided tangents at the\ntwo ends of each piece are future timelike; the definition is applied to a map\non an arbitrary interval $I$ with nonempty interior by requiring it on every\ncompact subinterval of $I$. A future timelike velocity is in particular\nnonzero, so every future timelike worldline is regular.\n\n**(d) Proper time and length.** Let $\\gamma:[a,b]\\to M$ be a piecewise-$C^1$\nfuture timelike worldline and let $a=s_0<s_1<\\cdots<s_m=b$ be an admissible\npartition. On each open piece $(s_{i-1},s_i)$ the function\n\n$$h_i(s):=\\sqrt{-g_{\\gamma(s)}\\bigl(\\gamma'(s),\\gamma'(s)\\bigr)}$$\n\nis continuous and strictly positive: it is a finite sum of products of\ncontinuous real functions in any chart around a point of the piece, as\n$g_{ab}\\circ\\gamma$ and the components of $\\gamma'$ are continuous\n([[thm-componentwise-limits-and-continuity]],\n[[thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions]], [[def-continuity-real]]), and the\nsquare root is continuous on $(0,\\infty)$ ([[def-rational-power]],\n[[thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives]]). The continuous one-sided limits of the component derivatives, required\nby the $C^1$ extension of each piece, and continuity of $g$ imply that\n$h_i$ tends to the norm of the corresponding endpoint tangent. The future\ntimelike hypothesis on those tangents makes these limits strictly positive, and $h_i$ extends to a continuous function on\n$[s_{i-1},s_i]$; hence it is Darboux integrable there\n([[def-darboux-integral]], [[thm-continuous-implies-integrable]]). The\n**proper-time increment** of $\\gamma$ over $[a,b]$ is\n\n$$\\tau(b)-\\tau(a):=\\frac{1}{c}\\sum_{i=1}^{m}\\int_{s_{i-1}}^{s_i}h_i(s)\\,ds,$$\n\nand the **Lorentzian length** of $\\gamma$ over $[a,b]$ is\n$L_g(\\gamma):=c\\,(\\tau(b)-\\tau(a))$. For a base point $s_0\\in I$ the\n**proper-time function** $\\tau:I\\to\\mathbb R$ of a future timelike worldline is\nthe function with $\\tau(s_0)=0$ and $\\tau(s)$ equal to the increment\nfrom $s_0$ to $s$ when $s>s_0$, and minus the increment from $s$ to $s_0$\nwhen $s<s_0$. That the increment is independent of the admissible\npartition, so that $\\tau$ is well defined, and that it is invariant under\nincreasing $C^1$ reparametrization with strictly positive derivative\n(including one-sided endpoint derivatives), comparing the increment over the\nactual image interval, are proved in\n[[lem-gr-proper-time-reparametrization-and-four-velocity]]; the display is the\nvalue for any admissible partition.\n\n**(e) Four-velocity.** Let $\\gamma$ be a piecewise-$C^1$ future timelike\nworldline and let $t$ be a parameter value at which $\\gamma$ is differentiable\n(interior point of a piece, or an included endpoint with its one-sided\ntangent). The **four-velocity** of $\\gamma$ at $t$ is\n\n$$U(t):=\\frac{c\\,\\gamma'(t)}{\\sqrt{-g_{\\gamma(t)}\\bigl(\\gamma'(t),\\gamma'(t)\\bigr)}}\\in T_{\\gamma(t)}M .$$\n\nBy construction $g_{\\gamma(t)}(U(t),U(t))=-c^2$, and\n$\\gamma'(t)=\\bigl(\\sqrt{-g(\\gamma',\\gamma')}/c\\bigr)U(t)$ with a strictly\npositive scalar factor. If $U$ is written as the derivative of $\\gamma$ with\nrespect to proper time, $U=d\\gamma/d\\tau$, this is a statement proved in\n[[lem-gr-proper-time-reparametrization-and-four-velocity]], not part of the\npresent definition.\n\n**(f) Scope.** Proper time, length and four-velocity are defined only for\nfuture timelike worldlines. A null or spacelike curve receives no proper time\nand no four-velocity from this definition, and no limiting or distributional\nextension of the square-root integrand to such curves is asserted. The\nparametrization of a worldline is part of the data and is arbitrary in this\ndefinition: no affine parameter, no geodesic equation, no completeness or\nextendability of the domain, and no measurement or clock reading is assumed.\nNull and spacelike regularity classes are not defined here beyond the general\n$C^k$ and piecewise-$C^1$ notions of clause (a), which make no causal\nrequirement.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-rpm-h-background-and-model-quantities",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice ACω, as in [[def-countable-choice]], for the smooth cotangent-bundle carrier convention used by this page; no full Axiom of Choice is assumed.\n\nFix a prescribed smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold $(M,g,T)$ with signature $(-+++)$, as in [[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]. Here $T$ denotes the supplied smooth timelike field selecting the future cone, called $N$ in that geometric definition. Use the distinction among a spacetime, a worldline, a pointwise observer, an observer congruence, a tetrad and a chart given in [[def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline]]. This definition does not make $g$ dynamical.\n\nAt each $x\\in M$, define $v^\\flat:=g_x(v,\\cdot)$ for $v\\in T_xM$. Nondegeneracy of $g_x$ makes $\\flat_x:T_xM\\to T_x^*M$ an isomorphism; write $p^\\sharp:=\\flat_x^{-1}(p)$ for $p\\in T_x^*M$, and define $g_x^{-1}(p,r):=g_x(p^\\sharp,r^\\sharp)$. In a chart, if $g_{ab}:=g(\\partial_a,\\partial_b)$ and $(g^{ab})$ is its inverse matrix, then $p^\\sharp=\\sum_{a,b=0}^3g^{ab}p_b\\partial_a$. A covector $p$ is **future causal** here exactly when $p^\\sharp$ is a nonzero future-causal vector in the sense of [[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]].\n\nIn this relativistic-particle model, $m>0$ is a fixed **primitive mass parameter** measured in kilograms, and $c>0$ is a fixed **primitive conversion parameter** measured in metres per second. They are parameters of the adopted physical formulation, not quantities derived by this definition. Coordinates $x^a$ in an SI chart have length units; a cotangent vector $p=p_a\\,dx^a$ has units of momentum, $\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$, when all four coordinates are in metres. The canonical one-form $p_a\\,dx^a$ therefore has action units $\\mathrm{J\\,s}$. Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval with nonempty interior and let $\\gamma:I\\to M$ be a $C^1$ curve; let $\\lambda$ be its real parameter and write $V=d\\gamma/d\\lambda$. When $V$ is future timelike, set $s:=\\sqrt{-g(V,V)}$ and define the proper-time density by $d\\tau=s\\,d\\lambda/c$. The tangent is then nonzero. If $\\widetilde\\lambda=f(\\lambda)$ is an increasing $C^1$ reparametrization with $f'(\\lambda)>0$, then $s_{\\widetilde\\lambda}=s_\\lambda/f'(\\lambda)$, so $s_{\\widetilde\\lambda}\\,d\\widetilde\\lambda=s_\\lambda\\,d\\lambda$. The length action $-mc\\int s\\,d\\lambda$ has action units. For a future unit observer $n$ and future causal covector $p$, define the model normal-observer energy and spatial momentum covector by\n\n$$E_n(p):=-c\\,p(n),\\qquad q_n(p):=p+p(n)n^\\flat,$$\n\nwhere $n^\\flat=g(n,\\cdot)$. We have $q_n(p)(n)=0$; write $n^\\perp:=\\{v\\in T_xM:g_x(v,n)=0\\}$. To verify the norm convention, choose a $g_x$-orthonormal basis $(e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ with $e_0$ future-directed (replace $e_0$ by $-e_0$ if necessary), and write $n=n^0e_0+\\sum_{i=1}^3n^ie_i$ and $v=v^0e_0+\\sum_{i=1}^3v^ie_i$. Use $n_s\\cdot v_s:=\\sum_{i=1}^3n^iv^i$, $|n_s|^2:=\\sum_{i=1}^3(n^i)^2$, and $|v_s|^2:=\\sum_{i=1}^3(v^i)^2$. The future-cone convention gives $n^0>|n_s|$, and $g_x(n,n)=-1$ gives $(n^0)^2=1+|n_s|^2$. If $g_x(v,n)=0$, then $v^0=(n_s\\cdot v_s)/n^0$, so\n\n$$g_x(v,v)=|v_s|^2-\\frac{(n_s\\cdot v_s)^2}{(n^0)^2}\\ge\\frac{|v_s|^2}{(n^0)^2}.$$\n\nHere $|n_s|^2|v_s|^2-(n_s\\cdot v_s)^2=\\sum_{1\\le i<j\\le3}(n^iv^j-n^jv^i)^2\\ge0$; if $v_s=0$, orthogonality also gives $v^0=0$. Thus $g_x$ is positive definite on $n^\\perp$, and the isomorphism $\\sharp_x$ carries covectors annihilating $n$ onto $n^\\perp$, so $g_x^{-1}$ is positive definite on that covector subspace. Define $\\|q_n(p)\\|_n:=\\sqrt{g_x^{-1}(q_n(p),q_n(p))}$. These are quantity definitions and unit conventions; they do not identify canonical momentum with mechanical momentum, assert that an apparatus measured these values, or establish a dynamical law.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-rpm-h-homogeneous-massive-shell",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g,T)$ be a smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold. At $x\\in M$, for $p\\in T_x^*M$, define $p^\\sharp$ as the unique vector satisfying $g_x(p^\\sharp,W)=p(W)$ for every $W\\in T_xM$, and set $g_x^{-1}(p,r):=g_x(p^\\sharp,r^\\sharp)$. In a chart, $p^\\sharp=g^{ab}p_b\\partial_a$, where $(g^{ab})$ is the inverse matrix of $g_{ab}=g(\\partial_a,\\partial_b)$. A covector on this background is future when its metric dual is future-directed. Let $m,c>0$ and $L(x,V)=-mc\\sqrt{-g(V,V)}$ on the future timelike tangent cone. Its fibre derivative is\n\n$$p_a=mc\\frac{g_{ab}V^b}{s},\\qquad s=\\sqrt{-g(V,V)}>0.$$\n\nThe velocity Hessian is $mc(g_{ab}/s+V_aV_b/s^3)$, has kernel exactly $\\mathbb RV$, and rank three. The image consists exactly of the future shell $\\Sigma_m^+=\\{(x,p)\\in T^*M:g_x^{-1}(p,p)=-m^2c^2,\\ p^\\sharp\\text{ future}\\}$. Each fibre is a connected positive ray, and the Legendre map is a surjective submersion onto this embedded image. Its energy $p(V)-L$ is zero; the map has no local inverse in the four-velocity direction.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Adopt as physical assumptions of the relativistic particle model, on top of the\nflat spacetime model of [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]] and the SI unit\nconventions of [[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]], together with\nthe framework's idealisations of point particles, ideal clocks and ideal light\nsignals:\n\n**(i) Mass and momentum.** Every massive classical particle of the model\ncarries a positive constant invariant mass parameter $m>0$ and has\nfour-momentum $P=mU$, where $U$ is the four-velocity of its future timelike\nworldline ([[def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline]],\n[[def-sr-four-velocity-and-four-acceleration]],\n[[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]]); $P$ is a vector of $V$\nwhose SI unit is kilogram metre per second. The formula is adopted only for\n$m>0$; the case $m=0$ is not defined by it and is the separate premise\n[[post-sr-ideal-massless-particle-momentum]]. In particular $\\eta(P,P)=-m^2c^2$\nis a consequence of the four-velocity normalisation, not a separate\nassumption.\n\n**(ii) Observer measurement identification.** Relative to a future unit\nobserver $n$, the model identifies the measured energy of the particle with\n$E_n(P)$, its measured spatial momentum with $q_n(P)$, its kinetic energy with\n$T_n(P)=E_n(P)-mc^2$ and its rest energy with $mc^2$, where $E_n,q_n,T_n$ are\nthe quantities of [[def-sr-observed-energy-momentum-and-kinetic-energy]]; the\nunits are the joule, kilogram metre per second and kilogram of\n[[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]]. This clause is a formulation\nconvention identifying mathematical quantities with the quantities the model\ncalls measured; it is not a measurement report and no experiment is claimed.\n\n**(iii) Isolated collision balance.** An isolated collision is represented by\na mass-specified on-shell collision datum at an event, with finite nonempty\nincoming and outgoing lists of the massive particles of (i) and/or the ideal\nmassless particles of [[post-sr-ideal-massless-particle-momentum]], and with\nno external four-momentum transfer or omitted leg. The interaction is\nidealized as confined to that event. The sum of the incoming four-momenta equals the sum of the\noutgoing four-momenta, in the sense of the collision data of\n[[def-sr-collision-datum-and-conservation]]: the model adopts\n$Q_{\\mathrm{in}}=Q_{\\mathrm{out}}$ for such an isolated datum. No particular\ncollision is asserted to occur, and no mechanism, probability, cross section\nor amplitude is supplied by this clause.\n\n**(iv) Equation of motion for a stipulated force.** Put\n$H_c^+:=\\{U\\in C^+:\\eta(U,U)=-c^2\\}$. A stipulated force law is a given\nmap $F:D\\to V$ on a given subset $D\\subseteq A\\times H_c^+$,\nwith force components in $\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm m/\\mathrm s^2$. No regularity\naway from the specified worldline is assumed. Along a given $C^2$ massive worldline\n$x:I\\to A$ with $(x(s),U(s))\\in D$ for all $s\\in I$, the model's particle\nequation is $m\\,dU/d\\tau=F(x,U)$, with\n$dU/d\\tau$ the proper-time derivative of\n[[def-sr-proper-time-derivative-and-four-force]], so that the stipulated $F$\nis the four-force of the motion. A particular force law, and the existence,\nuniqueness and domain of solutions of the resulting equation, are not adopted\nhere and belong to a separate model.\n\nEmpirical support for these assumptions is a nonlogical relation of the\nframework, not a proof; the idealisations of point particles, ideal clocks,\nideal light signals and flat spacetime are inherited and travel to every\nconsumer of this premise.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "pthm-sr-momentum-and-four-force",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Adopt [[post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics]] and\n[[post-sr-ideal-massless-particle-momentum]]. Then the following conditional\nphysical claims hold in the flat classical model, with the units of\n[[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]].\n\n**(a) Massive particle.** Let a massive particle have constant mass $m>0$,\nfour-momentum $P=mU$ and a future unit observer $n$. Its measured energy\n$E_n(P)=-c\\,\\eta(P,n)$, spatial momentum $q_n(P)=h(P)$ and kinetic energy\n$T_n(P)=E_n(P)-mc^2$ satisfy $E_n(P)>0$, $T_n(P)\\ge0$ and the mass-shell\nidentity $E_n(P)^2=m^2c^4+c^2|q_n(P)|^2$; $T_n(P)=0$ if and only if the\nparticle is at rest in the observer's frame ($U=cn$). In an inertial frame\nwhose stationary observer is $n=e_0$, the components are $P=(E/c,\\mathbf p)$\nwith $E=E_{e_0}(P)=m\\gamma_wc^2$ and $\\mathbf p=m\\gamma_w\\mathbf w\\in\\mathbb R^3$\nthe coordinates of $q_{e_0}(P)=\\sum_{i=1}^3p^ie_i$\nfor the coordinate velocity $\\mathbf w$, and under a passive boost the\ncomponents transform as the components of the vector $P$; the combination\n$E^2-c^2|\\mathbf p|^2=m^2c^4$ is frame-independent.\n\n**(b) Four-force.** Along a $C^2$ worldline with four-acceleration $A$, the\nfour-force $F=\\dot P$ satisfies $\\eta(P,F)=0$ and $F=mA$; in an inertial frame\n$F=\\gamma\\,(\\mathbf f\\cdot\\mathbf w/c,\\ \\mathbf f)$ and\n$dE/dt=\\mathbf f\\cdot\\mathbf w$, where $\\mathbf f=d\\mathbf p/dt$ is the\ncoordinate three-force. The constancy of $m$ is used; if instead $m$ is a\n$C^1$ function $m:I\\to(0,\\infty)$ along the same worldline, define $P:=mU$\nand $F:=\\dot P$ by the general derivative construction. As a mathematical identity, $\\eta(P,F)=-mc^2\\,dm/d\\tau$, and the\nconstant-mass identities are not transferred to variable-mass systems.\n\n**(c) System mass.** For a finite nonempty system of particles all of whose\nfour-momenta are nonzero future causal, let $Q:=\\sum_iP_i$. If $Q$ is\nfuture timelike, its invariant mass $M=\\sqrt{-\\eta(Q,Q)}/c$ satisfies: in the\ncenter-of-momentum frame with $e_0=Q/(Mc)$ one has\n$Mc^2=\\sum_iE_{e_0}(P_i)$; every massive constituent has\n$E_{e_0}(P_i)\\ge m_ic^2$, with equality exactly when it is at rest in that\nframe, and every massless constituent has $E_{e_0}(P_i)>0$. Hence\n$M\\ge\\sum_im_i$ over the massive constituents, with equality if and only if\nthe system is massive-only and all its constituents are at rest in the\ncenter-of-momentum frame. If $Q$ is future null, then $M=0$, all constituents\nare massless with parallel momenta, and no center-of-momentum frame exists.\n\n**(d) Massless particle.** An ideal massless particle has\n$E_n(P)=c\\,|q_n(P)|>0$, has no rest frame and is not $mU$ for any $m>0$; it is\nnot obtained from the massive formula by setting $m=0$.\n\n**(e) Necessary collision thresholds.** Suppose that, under the adopted\nisolated-collision balance, an isolated collision occurs whose incoming legs\nare massive particles of constant masses $m_1,\\dots,m_N$ and whose outgoing\nlegs are massive particles of masses $\\mu_1,\\dots,\\mu_K$ and possibly ideal\nmassless particles. Let $M$ be the invariant mass of the common total\nfour-momentum $Q$. Then the necessary inequalities $M\\ge\\sum_im_i$ and\n$M\\ge\\sum_j\\mu_j$ hold; if the incoming side is a projectile of mass $m_1$\nand a target of mass $m_2$ at rest in the observer frame of the target, with\nprojectile energy $E$ and kinetic energy $T=E-m_1c^2$ in that frame, then\n\n$$E\\ \\ge\\ \\frac{c^2}{2m_2}\\Bigl[\\Bigl(\\sum_{j=1}^K\\mu_j\\Bigr)^2-m_1^2-m_2^2\\Bigr],\\qquad T\\ \\ge\\ \\frac{c^2}{2m_2}\\Bigl[\\Bigl(\\sum_{j=1}^K\\mu_j\\Bigr)^2-(m_1+m_2)^2\\Bigr],$$\n\nSeparately, under the same adopted balance, a single incoming massless particle\ncannot produce an outgoing state containing a massive particle. These are kinematic necessity statements under\nthe adopted conservation: they do not assert that any collision occurs, do\nnot fix probabilities, amplitudes or cross sections, and are not empirical\nfindings.\n\n**(f) Scope.** All clauses are conditional on the two postulates and on the\nstated idealisations; the conclusions carry the same idealisations. No\nmeasurement, precision, statistical or empirical claim is made.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $c > 0$ and let $(A,V,\\eta,C^{+})$ be an oriented time-oriented affine\nMinkowski space ([[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]]).\n\n**(a) Norm of the four-velocity.** Let $x : I \\to A$ be a $C^{1}$ future\ntimelike worldline with four-velocity $U$\n([[def-sr-four-velocity-and-four-acceleration]]). Then\n$\\eta(U(s),U(s)) = -c^{2}$ for every $s \\in I$; equivalently the dimensionless\nfour-velocity $u = U/c$ satisfies $\\eta(u,u) = -1$, and $U(s)$ is future\ntimelike. Thus the dimensionless four-velocity lies on the future unit\nhyperboloid $\\{v \\in C^{+}:\\eta(v,v)=-1\\}$.\n\n**(b) Orthogonality of the four-acceleration.** If $x$ is $C^{2}$ at $s$, then\n$\\eta(U(s),A(s)) = 0$: the four-acceleration has no component along the\nfour-velocity; if in addition $A(s) \\ne 0$, then $A(s)$ is spacelike.\n\n**(c) Rest space and projection.** Let $n \\in V$ be future unit timelike,\n$\\eta(n,n) = -1$, and put $n^{\\perp} := \\{\\,v \\in V : \\eta(n,v) = 0\\,\\}$\n([[def-lorentz-orthogonality-and-orthogonal-complement]]). Then $n^{\\perp}$ is a\n3-dimensional linear subspace of $V$, the restriction of $\\eta$ to $n^{\\perp}$\nis positive definite, and the map $h : V \\to V$, $h(v) := v + \\eta(n,v)\\,n$, is\nlinear, idempotent with $h \\circ h = h$, has image $n^{\\perp}$ and kernel\n$\\operatorname{span}(n)$, satisfies $h(n) = 0$ and $\\eta(n,h(v)) = 0$ for all\n$v \\in V$, and every $v \\in V$ has the unique decomposition\n$v = -\\eta(n,v)\\,n + h(v)$. Moreover $|w| := \\sqrt{\\eta(w,w)}$ defines a norm\non $n^{\\perp}$.\n\n**(d) Relative velocity and Lorentz factor.** Let $n$ and $u$ be future unit\ntimelike vectors, put $\\gamma := -\\eta(n,u)$ and $w := c\\,h(u)/\\gamma$. Then\n$\\gamma \\ge 1$, $w$ lies in $n^{\\perp}$,\n$u = \\gamma\\,(n + w/c)$, $|w|^{2} = c^{2}(1-\\gamma^{-2}) < c^{2}$,\n$\\gamma = (1-|w|^{2}/c^{2})^{-1/2}$, and $\\gamma = 1$ if and only if $u = n$.\nThe vector $w$ is the velocity of a worldline with four-velocity $cu$ relative\nto the observer $n$, and $\\gamma$ is its Lorentz factor.\n\n**(e) Coordinate form.** Let $(o;e_0,\\dots,e_3)$ be an inertial frame and let\n$y$ be a $C^{1}$ future timelike worldline whose inertial coordinates are\n$t \\mapsto (ct,r(t))$, with coordinate velocity $w(t) := r'(t)$ in\n$\\mathbb{R}^{3}$ and, when $y$ is $C^{2}$, coordinate acceleration\n$a(t) := w'(t)$. Then $|w(t)| < c$, the four-velocity is\n$U = \\gamma_w\\,(c,w)$ with $\\gamma_w := (1-|w|^{2}/c^{2})^{-1/2}$, and the\nfour-acceleration is $A = \\gamma_w\\,\\frac{d}{dt}\\bigl(\\gamma_w(c,w)\\bigr)$, so\nthat\n\n$$A^{0} = \\gamma_w^{4}\\,\\frac{w \\cdot a}{c}, \\qquad A_{\\mathrm{vec}} = \\gamma_w^{2}\\,a + \\gamma_w^{4}\\,\\frac{(w \\cdot a)\\,w}{c^{2}} ;$$\n\nconsequently\n$\\eta(A,A) = \\gamma_w^{4}|a|^{2} + \\gamma_w^{6}(w \\cdot a)^{2}/c^{2} \\ge 0$,\nwith $\\eta(A,A) = 0$ if and only if $a = 0$, and $\\eta(U,A) = 0$. For the\nobserver $n = e_0$ of the chart, clauses (c) and (d) specialize to\n$\\gamma = \\gamma_w$ and $w_{\\mathrm{rel}} = (0,w)$ in the frame coordinates, identified with $w$ via the spatial basis.\n\n**(f) Scope.** The statement is a theorem about the affine Minkowski model with\na fixed positive constant $c$; it asserts no clock reading and identifies no\nphysical four-velocity. No choice principle is used.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "post-rpm-h-free-test-particle-action",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Let $I\\subset\\mathbb R$ be an open interval, and let $x:I\\to M$ be a $C^2$ future-directed timelike worldline on a prescribed smooth, time-oriented Lorentzian background $(M,g,T)$ of signature $(-+++)$, with $m>0$ a fixed primitive mass in kg and $c>0$ in m/s. For any compact segment $[a,b]\\subset I$ with $a<b$, adopt the stationary-action model on that segment\n\n$$S_{m,[a,b]}[x]=-mc\\int_a^b\\sqrt{-g(x',x') }\\,d\\lambda.$$\n\nHere $I\\subset\\mathbb R$ is an open interval, $\\lambda$ is its real coordinate, and $x'$ denotes $dx/d\\lambda$. The future-timelike condition makes $x'$ nowhere zero. On a neighborhood of $[a,b]$, $x$ is $C^2$ (so its tangent is $C^1$). If $\\widetilde\\lambda=f(\\lambda)$ is an increasing $C^1$ reparametrization with $f'>0$, then $\\sqrt{-g(dx/d\\widetilde\\lambda,dx/d\\widetilde\\lambda)}\\,d\\widetilde\\lambda=\\sqrt{-g(x',x')}\\,d\\lambda$ by the chain rule. An admissible variation is a one-parameter family $x_\\epsilon(\\lambda)$ that is $C^2$ jointly in $(\\epsilon,\\lambda)$, has fixed endpoint events $x_\\epsilon(a)=x(a)$ and $x_\\epsilon(b)=x(b)$, and remains future timelike for sufficiently small $|\\epsilon|$. The integrand is the proper-time length density, so $S_{m,[a,b]}=-mc^2\\int_a^b d\\tau$; its dimension is action (J s). The adopted premise concerns this ideal free test body only. It is neither a theorem about all matter nor an empirical report, and supplies no backreaction or completeness claim. The flat ideal-clock specialization agrees with [[post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light]]; adopting this action on a curved background remains a separate premise.\n\nThe cotangent phase-space carrier convention assumes countable choice ACω only; full choice is not assumed.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-rpm-h-first-order-action-and-geodesics",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g,T)$ be a prescribed smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold with signature $(-+++)$. Work in a spacetime chart on a compact parameter interval $[a,b]$ with $a<b$; take $x,p,e$ smooth on a neighbourhood of that interval, $e>0$, $m\\ge0$, and $c>0$. Select stationary triples on the future shell branch after taking the independent variations: when $m>0$, $p^\\sharp$ is future timelike; when $m=0$, require $p\\ne0$ and $p^\\sharp$ future null. Set $C_m=\\tfrac12(g^{ab}p_ap_b+m^2c^2)$ and define the first-order functional\n\n$$I_m[x,p,e]=\\int_a^b\\left(p_ax'^a-eC_m(x,p)\\right)d\\lambda.$$\n\nTake independent smooth variations of $p,e$ and fixed-endpoint variations of $x$. Stationarity on the chart segment is equivalent to\n\n$$x'^a=e g^{ab}p_b,\\quad p'_a=-\\tfrac e2\\partial_a g^{bc}p_bp_c,\\quad C_m=0.$$\n\nOn the massive future branch $m>0$, these equations are equivalent to stationary paths of the mathematical square-root functional $S[x]=-mc\\int_a^b\\sqrt{-g(x\\prime,x\\prime)}\\,d\\lambda$ on future-timelike curves with fixed endpoints; the fibre-derivative covector is $p=mc\\,x'^\\flat/\\sqrt{-g(x',x')}$, and proper-time gauge has $e=1/m$. For the separate nonzero future-null branch $m=0$, the equations describe a null geodesic with an arbitrary positive parameter multiplier; no proper-time parameter exists. In both cases the unparameterized path is a geodesic, and no existence or completeness claim is made.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Use the inherited smooth tangent bundle structure under [A1], adopt the square-root model [F4], and use the proper-time convention [F1]. Put $s=\\sqrt{-g(V,V)}>0$. The proper-time definition gives $d\\tau/d\\lambda=s/c$, so $U=V/(d\\tau/d\\lambda)=cV/s$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
