    "thm-rpm-h-temporal-shell-reduction": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-rpm-h-temporal-chart-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice ACω, as in [[def-countable-choice]], for the smooth cotangent-bundle carrier convention used by this page; no full Axiom of Choice is assumed.\n\nLet $(M,g,T)$ be a smooth, time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold of signature $(-+++)$, with the geometric meaning fixed by [[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]. Here $T$ denotes the future-cone field called $N$ in that definition. Let $I\\subset\\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open interval, let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ be open, and let $U\\subset M$ be open. A **temporal product chart** is a smooth diffeomorphism $\\chi:U\\to I\\times\\Omega$. Its coordinate functions $x^0,x^1,x^2,x^3$ are the four component functions of $\\chi$. Spatial indices $i,j$ range over $1,2,3$, spacetime indices $a,b$ range over $0,1,2,3$, and repeated upper/lower indices are summed. We require $x^0$ and each $x^i$ to have length units in the physical interpretation.\n\nThe chart has **lapse-shift data** $(N,\\beta,h)$ when $N:I\\times\\Omega\\to(0,\\infty)$, $\\beta^i:I\\times\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$, and $h_{ij}:I\\times\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ are smooth, $h_{ij}=h_{ji}$, and the matrix $(h_{ij})$ is positive definite at each point, with\n\n$$g=-N^2(dx^0)^2+h_{ij}(dx^i+\\beta^i dx^0)(dx^j+\\beta^j dx^0).$$\n\nWrite $h^{ij}$ for the inverse matrix. The normal field is\n\n$$n=N^{-1}(\\partial_0-\\beta^i\\partial_i).$$\n\nDirect contraction gives $g(n,n)=-1$; require that $n$ is future-directed in the supplied time orientation. This field of observers is distinct from the coordinate vector $\\partial_0$, which may be spacelike. The coefficients $N,\\beta^i,h_{ij}$ are dimensionless when all four coordinates are in metres. This definition is local to the specified product chart; it asserts no global temporal foliation.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a smooth four-manifold without boundary, so that $M$ is Hausdorff\nand second countable and carries a maximal smooth atlas\n([[def-smooth-manifold]]). Let $g$ be a smooth covariant $2$-tensor field on $M$\nin the sense of [[def-gr-smooth-covariant-two-tensor-field]]. Then $g$ assigns\nto every $p\\in M$ a bilinear form $g_p:T_pM\\times T_pM\\to\\mathbb R$\n([[def-derivation-at-a-point-and-tangent-space]]).\n\n**(a) Lorentzian metric.** The field $g$ is a **Lorentzian metric of signature\n$(-,+,+,+)$** on $M$ when it is symmetric and nondegenerate, and at every\n$p\\in M$ the pair $(T_pM,g_p)$ is a Lorentz vector space of signature\n$(-,+,+,+)$ in the sense of [[def-lorentz-form-and-inertia]]: that is, $g_p$ has\ninertia $(3,1,0)$, so that some — equivalently every — basis of $T_pM$ in which\nthe matrix of $g_p$ is diagonal has exactly one negative and three positive\ndiagonal entries. The pair $(M,g)$ is a **smooth Lorentzian four-manifold** (or\nLorentzian manifold of signature $(-,+,+,+)$). In a smooth chart $(U,x)$ the\n**components** of $g$ are the $C^\\infty$ functions\n$g_{ab}(q)=g_q(\\partial_a|_q,\\partial_b|_q)$ of\n[[def-gr-smooth-covariant-two-tensor-field]], and $g_{ab}=g_{ba}$ by symmetry.\nWe use indices $a,b,c,d=0,1,2,3$ and the Einstein summation convention, with\ncomponents in a chart written as lower-case indexed symbols and equations\nbetween indexed fields understood basis-independently. These conventions are\nfixed once for this library and the physical reading of the symbols is recorded\nwith the framework postulates, separately from this mathematical definition.\n\n**(b) Pointwise causal structure.** For every $p\\in M$ the vocabulary of\n[[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]] applies to the Lorentz vector\nspace $(T_pM,g_p)$: a tangent vector $v\\in T_pM$ is **timelike** when\n$g_p(v,v)<0$, **null** when $v\\neq0$ and $g_p(v,v)=0$, **spacelike** when\n$g_p(v,v)>0$, and **causal** when it is timelike or null. Every nonzero\n$v\\in T_pM$ has exactly one causal character. The **timelike set**\n$T_p:=\\{v\\in T_pM:g_p(v,v)<0\\}$ has exactly two connected components, each\nnonempty, open, convex and path-connected; each $g_p$-orthonormal basis\n$e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3$ of $T_pM$ satisfies $g_p(e_a,e_b)=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)_{ab}$\nfor some ordering, so in particular nonzero orthonormal vectors exist at every\npoint ([[lem-lorentz-form-has-orthonormal-basis]],\n[[lem-timelike-cone-has-two-convex-components]]).\n\n**(c) Time orientation.** A **time orientation** of $(M,g)$, also called a\n**choice of future cone**, is a smooth vector field $N$ on $M$\n([[def-smooth-vector-field-as-a-tangent-bundle-section]], whose tangent-bundle\nsmooth structure uses the axiom $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ of\n[[def-countable-choice]]; this is the only use of that axiom in this\ndefinition) such that\n\n$$g_p(N_p,N_p)<0\\qquad\\text{for every }p\\in M .$$\n\nThe function $p\\mapsto g_p(N_p,N_p)$ is $C^\\infty$ because $g$ is a smooth\ncovariant $2$-tensor field and $N$ a smooth field, so the condition is a\npointwise strict inequality between a smooth function and $0$; since\n$g_p(N_p,N_p)<0$ implies $N_p\\neq0$, a time orientation is nowhere vanishing.\nA **time-oriented smooth Lorentzian four-manifold** is a triple $(M,g,N)$\nconsisting of a smooth Lorentzian four-manifold and a time orientation $N$.\n\nGiven a time orientation $N$ and $p\\in M$, the vector $N_p$ is timelike, and we\n**define** a nonzero causal vector $v\\in T_pM$ to be **future-directed** when\n$g_p(v,N_p)<0$ and **past-directed** when $g_p(v,N_p)>0$. The classification of\nnonzero causal vectors into exactly these two classes, and its agreement with\nthe two components of the timelike set (the future component is the component\n$C_p^+$ containing $N_p$, and the future causal vectors form\n$\\operatorname{cl}(C_p^+)\\setminus\\{0\\}$), is proved in\n[[lem-gr-future-causal-classification-by-a-timelike-field]]; in particular no\nnonzero causal $v$ satisfies $g_p(v,N_p)=0$, so the definition is exhaustive and\nmutually exclusive. Replacing $N$ by $-N$ reverses every future/past label, and\ntwo time orientations $N,N'$ determine the same future-directed causal vectors\nat every point where $g_p(N_p,N'_p)<0$, while they determine opposite ones\nwhere $g_p(N_p,N'_p)>0$, by\n[[lem-gr-timelike-fields-define-equal-or-opposite-future-cones]]; that lemma\nalso shows the sign of $p\\mapsto g_p(N_p,N'_p)$ is constant on every connected\ncomponent of $M$.\n\n**(d) The inverse metric.** Because $g_p$ is nondegenerate, it induces at each\n$p$ an isomorphism $\\flat_p:T_pM\\to T_p^*M$, $v\\mapsto g_p(v,\\cdot)$, and an\ninverse metric $g_p^{-1}$ on the dual space; the inverse metric, the musical\nisomorphisms and the components $g^{ab}$ are defined separately on this page,\nwhere the smoothness of those components is also proved. Neither construction\nuses any choice principle beyond the one already recorded in (c).\n\n\n**(e) Smooth timelike curves and their scalar normalization.** Before using\nthese quantities in the physical conventions, fix a positive real scaling\nparameter $c$ (with no physical meaning or dimension in this mathematical\nitem). A **smooth future timelike curve** is a smooth map $\\gamma:I\\to M$\nfrom a nonempty open real interval $I$ ([[def-interval]],\n[[def-c-r-and-smooth-maps-between-smooth-manifolds]]) whose velocity derivation\n$\\gamma'(s)\\in T_{\\gamma(s)}M$\n([[def-velocity-derivation-of-a-smooth-curve]]) satisfies\n$g(\\gamma',\\gamma')<0$ and $g(\\gamma',N)<0$ at every $s\\in I$.\nIn a chart its components are $d(x^a\\circ\\gamma)/ds$, $a=0,1,2,3$.\nFor a supplied base point $s_0\\in I$ and additive constant $\\tau_0\\in\\mathbb R$\ndefine\n\n$$h(s):=\\sqrt{-g_{\\gamma(s)}(\\gamma'(s),\\gamma'(s))},\\qquad \\tau(s):=\\tau_0+\\frac1c\\int_{s_0}^{s}h(r)\\,dr,\\qquad U(s):=\\frac{c\\gamma'(s)}{h(s)}.$$\n\nThe integral is oriented when $s<s_0$. The function $\\tau$ is called\n**proper time** for this scaling and $U$ the **four-velocity** of the smooth\ncurve; its increment times $c$ is the Lorentzian length. The well-definedness\nproof below shows $\\tau'>0$, that $\\tau:I\\to\\tau(I)$ is a smooth\ndiffeomorphism, and that $U$ is the velocity of $\\gamma\\circ\\tau^{-1}$.\nThus $g(U,U)=-c^2$ and $U$ is future timelike. This introduces the smooth\ncase before any physical unit reading; the later worldline definition extends\nit to its stated $C^k$, endpoint and piecewise-$C^1$ classes. It asserts no\nclock coupling, motion law or physical value of $c$.\n\n**Convention and scope.** The physical reading of a smooth time-oriented\nLorentzian four-manifold as a classical spacetime model, and the domain in which\nthat reading is adopted, are recorded in the framework postulate\n`post-gr-spacetime-and-classical-domain`; the words spacetime and event carry no\nmathematical content beyond this definition, and this item asserts no claim\nabout nature. The dimension $4$ and the absence of boundary are part of the\ndata; nothing in the definition assumes that $M$ is nonempty or connected, and\nno completeness, extendibility or maximality property of $(M,g)$ is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-rpm-h-first-order-action-and-geodesics",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g,T)$ be a prescribed smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold with signature $(-+++)$. Work in a spacetime chart on a compact parameter interval $[a,b]$ with $a<b$; take $x,p,e$ smooth on a neighbourhood of that interval, $e>0$, $m\\ge0$, and $c>0$. Select stationary triples on the future shell branch after taking the independent variations: when $m>0$, $p^\\sharp$ is future timelike; when $m=0$, require $p\\ne0$ and $p^\\sharp$ future null. Set $C_m=\\tfrac12(g^{ab}p_ap_b+m^2c^2)$ and define the first-order functional\n\n$$I_m[x,p,e]=\\int_a^b\\left(p_ax'^a-eC_m(x,p)\\right)d\\lambda.$$\n\nTake independent smooth variations of $p,e$ and fixed-endpoint variations of $x$. Stationarity on the chart segment is equivalent to\n\n$$x'^a=e g^{ab}p_b,\\quad p'_a=-\\tfrac e2\\partial_a g^{bc}p_bp_c,\\quad C_m=0.$$\n\nOn the massive future branch $m>0$, these equations are equivalent to stationary paths of the mathematical square-root functional $S[x]=-mc\\int_a^b\\sqrt{-g(x\\prime,x\\prime)}\\,d\\lambda$ on future-timelike curves with fixed endpoints; the fibre-derivative covector is $p=mc\\,x'^\\flat/\\sqrt{-g(x',x')}$, and proper-time gauge has $e=1/m$. For the separate nonzero future-null branch $m=0$, the equations describe a null geodesic with an arbitrary positive parameter multiplier; no proper-time parameter exists. In both cases the unparameterized path is a geodesic, and no existence or completeness claim is made.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-rpm-h-canonical-poisson-and-flow",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $Q$ be a finite-dimensional smooth manifold as in [[def-rpm-h-cotangent-phase-space]], and write its cotangent phase space in induced coordinates $(q^i,p_i)$. With $\\omega=dq^i\\wedge dp_i$, define $X_H$ by $\\iota_{X_H}\\omega=dH$ and define the Poisson bracket by $\\{F,G\\}:=X_G(F)$. Then\n\n$$X_H=\\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial p_i}\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial q^i}-\\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial q^i}\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial p_i},\\qquad \\{F,G\\}=F_{q^i}G_{p_i}-F_{p_i}G_{q^i}.$$\n\nThe bracket is coordinate independent and makes smooth functions a Poisson algebra. If $H_t$ is smooth on an open time-dependent domain $D\\subset\\mathbb R\\times T^*Q$, each solution of $\\dot z=X_{H_t}(z)$ has local uniqueness and smooth dependence on initial data and parameters; on its actual interval,\n\n$$\\frac{d}{dt}F(t,z(t))=\\partial_tF+\\{F,H_t\\}.$$\n\nNo completeness or global flow is asserted. If $H$ is smooth, each local time-$t$ flow map preserves ω.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-a-regular-level-set-is-an-embedded-submanifold",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $F:M^m\\to N^n$ be smooth, let $q\\in N$ be a regular value, and assume\n$F^{-1}(q)$ is nonempty. Then $F^{-1}(q)$ is an embedded submanifold of\ncodimension $n$. Equivalently, it has dimension $m-n$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-gr-observer-pairing-with-future-causal-vectors",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be a time-oriented smooth Lorentzian four-manifold, let\n$p\\in M$, and let $n$ be an observer at $p$\n([[def-gr-observer-and-rest-space]]). For $w\\in T_pM$ write\n$h=h_n$ and $\\gamma(w):=-g_p(n,w)$.\n\n1. If $w$ is nonzero future causal, then $\\gamma(w)>0$.\n2. If $w$ is future unit timelike, then $\\gamma(w)\\ge1$, with\n   $\\gamma(w)=1$ if and only if $w=n$, and\n   $g_p(h(w),h(w))=\\gamma(w)^2-1$.\n3. If $w$ is unit timelike, then $w$ is future-directed if and only if\n   $g_p(n,w)<0$, and past-directed if and only if $g_p(n,w)>0$; in particular\n   $g_p(n,w)\\neq0$ for such $w$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Use the supplied smooth tangent/cotangent structures under [A1] and the Lorentzian temporal-chart data [F1,F2]. Expand the metric matrix and invert its block form. Direct multiplication yields $g^{00}=-N^{-2}$, $g^{0i}=\\beta^i/N^2$, $g^{ij}=h^{ij}-\\beta^i\\beta^j/N^2$. Therefore $$g^{ab}p_ap_b=-\\frac{(p_0-\\beta^ip_i)^2}{N^2}+h^{ij}p_ip_j.$$ The shell equation gives $p_0-\\beta^ip_i=\\pm N\\kappa$. The future normal $n=N^{-1}(\\partial_0-\\beta^i\\partial_i)$ has $p(n)=(p_0-\\beta^ip_i)/N<0$ by [F6] for a nonzero future-causal shell covector, so the future branch is the negative root $p_0=\\beta^ip_i-N\\kappa$. Positive $h$ implies κ>0 except the excluded massless zero momentum.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F6",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The fibre derivative of $C_m$ with respect to $p_0$ is $g^{0b}p_b=\\kappa/N>0$ on this branch. Thus the root is smooth and the shell is locally a graph over $(t,q^i,p_i)$; [F5] also gives the embedded regular-level structure.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "On $x^0=ct$, the canonical term is $p_0c+p_i\\dot q^i=p_i\\dot q^i-H$. The constraint term vanishes on the solved shell, so the reduced action has the claimed integrand.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Applying the fixed-endpoint first-variation equations to the reduced canonical action gives $\\dot q^i=\\partial H/\\partial p_i$, $\\dot p_i=-\\partial H/\\partial q^i$; by [F4] these are Hamilton's equations for this time-dependent chart Hamiltonian. Smooth root and $N>0$ ensure the gauge parameter $t=x^0/c$ is valid on the stated interval; nothing here makes it global outside the chart.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F4",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 2.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Statement — A curved temporal chart gives a local reduced Hamiltonian: The conclusion concerns supplied local or finite data rather than an aggregate construction over an empty index set. Strictly positive lapse and positive-definite spatial metric give positive kappa on the massive or nonzero-null future shell. The temporal gauge is transverse and its fixed-endpoint compact chart action reduces locally, without a global curved quotient.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $m\\ge0$ and $c>0$. Assume a temporal product chart with lapse-shift data $(N,\\beta,h)$ as in [[def-rpm-h-temporal-chart-data]], all four coordinates measured in metres. Write $g_{ab}=g(\\partial_a,\\partial_b)$ for $a,b\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}$ and let $g^{ab}$ denote the entries of the inverse matrix; then $p^\\sharp:=\\sum_{a,b=0}^3g^{ab}p_b\\partial_a$ is the unique vector satisfying $g(p^\\sharp,w)=p(w)$ for every tangent vector $w$. Put $T=\\{t\\in\\mathbb R:ct\\in I\\}$. In every reduced expression below, $N$, $\\beta^i$, $h_{ij}$ and $h^{ij}$ mean their pullbacks to $T\\times\\Omega$: $N(ct,q)$, $\\beta^i(ct,q)$, $h_{ij}(ct,q)$ and $h^{ij}(ct,q)$, respectively. Thus $\\kappa$, $p_0$ and $H$ are functions of $(t,q,p)$ on the massive reduced domain, or on its nonzero-spatial-momentum part when $m=0$; the spacetime metric and shell covector are evaluated at $(ct,q)$. On the future shell $C_m=\\tfrac12(g^{ab}p_ap_b+m^2c^2)=0$, define\n\n$$\\kappa=\\sqrt{m^2c^2+h^{ij}p_ip_j},\\quad p_0=\\beta^ip_i-N\\kappa,\\quad H(t,q,p)=-cp_0=c(N\\kappa-\\beta^ip_i).$$\n\nFor $m=0$, require $p_i\\ne0$. Let $J=[t_1,t_2]$ satisfy $t_1<t_2$ and $[ct_1,ct_2]\\subset I$, where $I$ is the first-coordinate interval of the temporal product chart; require the path $t\\mapsto(ct,q(t))$ to stay in the chart. Take $q^i,p_i$ smooth on a neighborhood of $J$ and vary them by smooth one-parameter families that keep the paths in the chart, fix $q$ at both endpoints and vary $p$ freely; in the massless case the paths and sufficiently small variations stay in $p_i\\ne0$. Then the gauge $x^0=ct$ reduces the first-order action to $\\int_{t_1}^{t_2}(p_i\\dot q^i-H)dt$, and its variational equations on the interior of $J$ are exactly $\\dot q^i=H_{p_i}$, $\\dot p_i=-H_{q^i}$. The construction is local to this chart and intervals transverse to its time slices; no global curved-space gauge or quotient is asserted.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "53f3fcbd8d4749e714ecc83d7fb336824b7e714dbb73e29b20388fe32da0e352",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement — A curved temporal chart gives a local reduced Hamiltonian: The permitted zero values and nonzero normalization domain are fixed by the declared input conditions. Strictly positive lapse and positive-definite spatial metric give positive kappa on the massive or nonzero-null future shell. The temporal gauge is transverse and its fixed-endpoint compact chart action reduces locally, without a global curved quotient.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $m\\ge0$ and $c>0$. Assume a temporal product chart with lapse-shift data $(N,\\beta,h)$ as in [[def-rpm-h-temporal-chart-data]], all four coordinates measured in metres. Write $g_{ab}=g(\\partial_a,\\partial_b)$ for $a,b\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}$ and let $g^{ab}$ denote the entries of the inverse matrix; then $p^\\sharp:=\\sum_{a,b=0}^3g^{ab}p_b\\partial_a$ is the unique vector satisfying $g(p^\\sharp,w)=p(w)$ for every tangent vector $w$. Put $T=\\{t\\in\\mathbb R:ct\\in I\\}$. In every reduced expression below, $N$, $\\beta^i$, $h_{ij}$ and $h^{ij}$ mean their pullbacks to $T\\times\\Omega$: $N(ct,q)$, $\\beta^i(ct,q)$, $h_{ij}(ct,q)$ and $h^{ij}(ct,q)$, respectively. Thus $\\kappa$, $p_0$ and $H$ are functions of $(t,q,p)$ on the massive reduced domain, or on its nonzero-spatial-momentum part when $m=0$; the spacetime metric and shell covector are evaluated at $(ct,q)$. On the future shell $C_m=\\tfrac12(g^{ab}p_ap_b+m^2c^2)=0$, define\n\n$$\\kappa=\\sqrt{m^2c^2+h^{ij}p_ip_j},\\quad p_0=\\beta^ip_i-N\\kappa,\\quad H(t,q,p)=-cp_0=c(N\\kappa-\\beta^ip_i).$$\n\nFor $m=0$, require $p_i\\ne0$. Let $J=[t_1,t_2]$ satisfy $t_1<t_2$ and $[ct_1,ct_2]\\subset I$, where $I$ is the first-coordinate interval of the temporal product chart; require the path $t\\mapsto(ct,q(t))$ to stay in the chart. Take $q^i,p_i$ smooth on a neighborhood of $J$ and vary them by smooth one-parameter families that keep the paths in the chart, fix $q$ at both endpoints and vary $p$ freely; in the massless case the paths and sufficiently small variations stay in $p_i\\ne0$. Then the gauge $x^0=ct$ reduces the first-order action to $\\int_{t_1}^{t_2}(p_i\\dot q^i-H)dt$, and its variational equations on the interior of $J$ are exactly $\\dot q^i=H_{p_i}$, $\\dot p_i=-H_{q^i}$. The construction is local to this chart and intervals transverse to its time slices; no global curved-space gauge or quotient is asserted.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "53f3fcbd8d4749e714ecc83d7fb336824b7e714dbb73e29b20388fe32da0e352",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
