    "pthm-rpm-h-coordinate-versus-observer-energy": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-rpm-h-background-and-model-quantities",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice ACω, as in [[def-countable-choice]], for the smooth cotangent-bundle carrier convention used by this page; no full Axiom of Choice is assumed.\n\nFix a prescribed smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold $(M,g,T)$ with signature $(-+++)$, as in [[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]. Here $T$ denotes the supplied smooth timelike field selecting the future cone, called $N$ in that geometric definition. Use the distinction among a spacetime, a worldline, a pointwise observer, an observer congruence, a tetrad and a chart given in [[def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline]]. This definition does not make $g$ dynamical.\n\nAt each $x\\in M$, define $v^\\flat:=g_x(v,\\cdot)$ for $v\\in T_xM$. Nondegeneracy of $g_x$ makes $\\flat_x:T_xM\\to T_x^*M$ an isomorphism; write $p^\\sharp:=\\flat_x^{-1}(p)$ for $p\\in T_x^*M$, and define $g_x^{-1}(p,r):=g_x(p^\\sharp,r^\\sharp)$. In a chart, if $g_{ab}:=g(\\partial_a,\\partial_b)$ and $(g^{ab})$ is its inverse matrix, then $p^\\sharp=\\sum_{a,b=0}^3g^{ab}p_b\\partial_a$. A covector $p$ is **future causal** here exactly when $p^\\sharp$ is a nonzero future-causal vector in the sense of [[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]].\n\nIn this relativistic-particle model, $m>0$ is a fixed **primitive mass parameter** measured in kilograms, and $c>0$ is a fixed **primitive conversion parameter** measured in metres per second. They are parameters of the adopted physical formulation, not quantities derived by this definition. Coordinates $x^a$ in an SI chart have length units; a cotangent vector $p=p_a\\,dx^a$ has units of momentum, $\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$, when all four coordinates are in metres. The canonical one-form $p_a\\,dx^a$ therefore has action units $\\mathrm{J\\,s}$. Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval with nonempty interior and let $\\gamma:I\\to M$ be a $C^1$ curve; let $\\lambda$ be its real parameter and write $V=d\\gamma/d\\lambda$. When $V$ is future timelike, set $s:=\\sqrt{-g(V,V)}$ and define the proper-time density by $d\\tau=s\\,d\\lambda/c$. The tangent is then nonzero. If $\\widetilde\\lambda=f(\\lambda)$ is an increasing $C^1$ reparametrization with $f'(\\lambda)>0$, then $s_{\\widetilde\\lambda}=s_\\lambda/f'(\\lambda)$, so $s_{\\widetilde\\lambda}\\,d\\widetilde\\lambda=s_\\lambda\\,d\\lambda$. The length action $-mc\\int s\\,d\\lambda$ has action units. For a future unit observer $n$ and future causal covector $p$, define the model normal-observer energy and spatial momentum covector by\n\n$$E_n(p):=-c\\,p(n),\\qquad q_n(p):=p+p(n)n^\\flat,$$\n\nwhere $n^\\flat=g(n,\\cdot)$. We have $q_n(p)(n)=0$; write $n^\\perp:=\\{v\\in T_xM:g_x(v,n)=0\\}$. To verify the norm convention, choose a $g_x$-orthonormal basis $(e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ with $e_0$ future-directed (replace $e_0$ by $-e_0$ if necessary), and write $n=n^0e_0+\\sum_{i=1}^3n^ie_i$ and $v=v^0e_0+\\sum_{i=1}^3v^ie_i$. Use $n_s\\cdot v_s:=\\sum_{i=1}^3n^iv^i$, $|n_s|^2:=\\sum_{i=1}^3(n^i)^2$, and $|v_s|^2:=\\sum_{i=1}^3(v^i)^2$. The future-cone convention gives $n^0>|n_s|$, and $g_x(n,n)=-1$ gives $(n^0)^2=1+|n_s|^2$. If $g_x(v,n)=0$, then $v^0=(n_s\\cdot v_s)/n^0$, so\n\n$$g_x(v,v)=|v_s|^2-\\frac{(n_s\\cdot v_s)^2}{(n^0)^2}\\ge\\frac{|v_s|^2}{(n^0)^2}.$$\n\nHere $|n_s|^2|v_s|^2-(n_s\\cdot v_s)^2=\\sum_{1\\le i<j\\le3}(n^iv^j-n^jv^i)^2\\ge0$; if $v_s=0$, orthogonality also gives $v^0=0$. Thus $g_x$ is positive definite on $n^\\perp$, and the isomorphism $\\sharp_x$ carries covectors annihilating $n$ onto $n^\\perp$, so $g_x^{-1}$ is positive definite on that covector subspace. Define $\\|q_n(p)\\|_n:=\\sqrt{g_x^{-1}(q_n(p),q_n(p))}$. These are quantity definitions and unit conventions; they do not identify canonical momentum with mechanical momentum, assert that an apparatus measured these values, or establish a dynamical law.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-rpm-h-temporal-chart-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice ACω, as in [[def-countable-choice]], for the smooth cotangent-bundle carrier convention used by this page; no full Axiom of Choice is assumed.\n\nLet $(M,g,T)$ be a smooth, time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold of signature $(-+++)$, with the geometric meaning fixed by [[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]. Here $T$ denotes the future-cone field called $N$ in that definition. Let $I\\subset\\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open interval, let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ be open, and let $U\\subset M$ be open. A **temporal product chart** is a smooth diffeomorphism $\\chi:U\\to I\\times\\Omega$. Its coordinate functions $x^0,x^1,x^2,x^3$ are the four component functions of $\\chi$. Spatial indices $i,j$ range over $1,2,3$, spacetime indices $a,b$ range over $0,1,2,3$, and repeated upper/lower indices are summed. We require $x^0$ and each $x^i$ to have length units in the physical interpretation.\n\nThe chart has **lapse-shift data** $(N,\\beta,h)$ when $N:I\\times\\Omega\\to(0,\\infty)$, $\\beta^i:I\\times\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$, and $h_{ij}:I\\times\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ are smooth, $h_{ij}=h_{ji}$, and the matrix $(h_{ij})$ is positive definite at each point, with\n\n$$g=-N^2(dx^0)^2+h_{ij}(dx^i+\\beta^i dx^0)(dx^j+\\beta^j dx^0).$$\n\nWrite $h^{ij}$ for the inverse matrix. The normal field is\n\n$$n=N^{-1}(\\partial_0-\\beta^i\\partial_i).$$\n\nDirect contraction gives $g(n,n)=-1$; require that $n$ is future-directed in the supplied time orientation. This field of observers is distinct from the coordinate vector $\\partial_0$, which may be spacelike. The coefficients $N,\\beta^i,h_{ij}$ are dimensionless when all four coordinates are in metres. This definition is local to the specified product chart; it asserts no global temporal foliation.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-rpm-h-temporal-shell-reduction",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $m\\ge0$ and $c>0$. Assume a temporal product chart with lapse-shift data $(N,\\beta,h)$ as in [[def-rpm-h-temporal-chart-data]], all four coordinates measured in metres. Write $g_{ab}=g(\\partial_a,\\partial_b)$ for $a,b\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}$ and let $g^{ab}$ denote the entries of the inverse matrix; then $p^\\sharp:=\\sum_{a,b=0}^3g^{ab}p_b\\partial_a$ is the unique vector satisfying $g(p^\\sharp,w)=p(w)$ for every tangent vector $w$. Put $T=\\{t\\in\\mathbb R:ct\\in I\\}$. In every reduced expression below, $N$, $\\beta^i$, $h_{ij}$ and $h^{ij}$ mean their pullbacks to $T\\times\\Omega$: $N(ct,q)$, $\\beta^i(ct,q)$, $h_{ij}(ct,q)$ and $h^{ij}(ct,q)$, respectively. Thus $\\kappa$, $p_0$ and $H$ are functions of $(t,q,p)$ on the massive reduced domain, or on its nonzero-spatial-momentum part when $m=0$; the spacetime metric and shell covector are evaluated at $(ct,q)$. On the future shell $C_m=\\tfrac12(g^{ab}p_ap_b+m^2c^2)=0$, define\n\n$$\\kappa=\\sqrt{m^2c^2+h^{ij}p_ip_j},\\quad p_0=\\beta^ip_i-N\\kappa,\\quad H(t,q,p)=-cp_0=c(N\\kappa-\\beta^ip_i).$$\n\nFor $m=0$, require $p_i\\ne0$. Let $J=[t_1,t_2]$ satisfy $t_1<t_2$ and $[ct_1,ct_2]\\subset I$, where $I$ is the first-coordinate interval of the temporal product chart; require the path $t\\mapsto(ct,q(t))$ to stay in the chart. Take $q^i,p_i$ smooth on a neighborhood of $J$ and vary them by smooth one-parameter families that keep the paths in the chart, fix $q$ at both endpoints and vary $p$ freely; in the massless case the paths and sufficiently small variations stay in $p_i\\ne0$. Then the gauge $x^0=ct$ reduces the first-order action to $\\int_{t_1}^{t_2}(p_i\\dot q^i-H)dt$, and its variational equations on the interior of $J$ are exactly $\\dot q^i=H_{p_i}$, $\\dot p_i=-H_{q^i}$. The construction is local to this chart and intervals transverse to its time slices; no global curved-space gauge or quotient is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "pthm-sr-momentum-and-four-force",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Adopt [[post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics]] and\n[[post-sr-ideal-massless-particle-momentum]]. Then the following conditional\nphysical claims hold in the flat classical model, with the units of\n[[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]].\n\n**(a) Massive particle.** Let a massive particle have constant mass $m>0$,\nfour-momentum $P=mU$ and a future unit observer $n$. Its measured energy\n$E_n(P)=-c\\,\\eta(P,n)$, spatial momentum $q_n(P)=h(P)$ and kinetic energy\n$T_n(P)=E_n(P)-mc^2$ satisfy $E_n(P)>0$, $T_n(P)\\ge0$ and the mass-shell\nidentity $E_n(P)^2=m^2c^4+c^2|q_n(P)|^2$; $T_n(P)=0$ if and only if the\nparticle is at rest in the observer's frame ($U=cn$). In an inertial frame\nwhose stationary observer is $n=e_0$, the components are $P=(E/c,\\mathbf p)$\nwith $E=E_{e_0}(P)=m\\gamma_wc^2$ and $\\mathbf p=m\\gamma_w\\mathbf w\\in\\mathbb R^3$\nthe coordinates of $q_{e_0}(P)=\\sum_{i=1}^3p^ie_i$\nfor the coordinate velocity $\\mathbf w$, and under a passive boost the\ncomponents transform as the components of the vector $P$; the combination\n$E^2-c^2|\\mathbf p|^2=m^2c^4$ is frame-independent.\n\n**(b) Four-force.** Along a $C^2$ worldline with four-acceleration $A$, the\nfour-force $F=\\dot P$ satisfies $\\eta(P,F)=0$ and $F=mA$; in an inertial frame\n$F=\\gamma\\,(\\mathbf f\\cdot\\mathbf w/c,\\ \\mathbf f)$ and\n$dE/dt=\\mathbf f\\cdot\\mathbf w$, where $\\mathbf f=d\\mathbf p/dt$ is the\ncoordinate three-force. The constancy of $m$ is used; if instead $m$ is a\n$C^1$ function $m:I\\to(0,\\infty)$ along the same worldline, define $P:=mU$\nand $F:=\\dot P$ by the general derivative construction. As a mathematical identity, $\\eta(P,F)=-mc^2\\,dm/d\\tau$, and the\nconstant-mass identities are not transferred to variable-mass systems.\n\n**(c) System mass.** For a finite nonempty system of particles all of whose\nfour-momenta are nonzero future causal, let $Q:=\\sum_iP_i$. If $Q$ is\nfuture timelike, its invariant mass $M=\\sqrt{-\\eta(Q,Q)}/c$ satisfies: in the\ncenter-of-momentum frame with $e_0=Q/(Mc)$ one has\n$Mc^2=\\sum_iE_{e_0}(P_i)$; every massive constituent has\n$E_{e_0}(P_i)\\ge m_ic^2$, with equality exactly when it is at rest in that\nframe, and every massless constituent has $E_{e_0}(P_i)>0$. Hence\n$M\\ge\\sum_im_i$ over the massive constituents, with equality if and only if\nthe system is massive-only and all its constituents are at rest in the\ncenter-of-momentum frame. If $Q$ is future null, then $M=0$, all constituents\nare massless with parallel momenta, and no center-of-momentum frame exists.\n\n**(d) Massless particle.** An ideal massless particle has\n$E_n(P)=c\\,|q_n(P)|>0$, has no rest frame and is not $mU$ for any $m>0$; it is\nnot obtained from the massive formula by setting $m=0$.\n\n**(e) Necessary collision thresholds.** Suppose that, under the adopted\nisolated-collision balance, an isolated collision occurs whose incoming legs\nare massive particles of constant masses $m_1,\\dots,m_N$ and whose outgoing\nlegs are massive particles of masses $\\mu_1,\\dots,\\mu_K$ and possibly ideal\nmassless particles. Let $M$ be the invariant mass of the common total\nfour-momentum $Q$. Then the necessary inequalities $M\\ge\\sum_im_i$ and\n$M\\ge\\sum_j\\mu_j$ hold; if the incoming side is a projectile of mass $m_1$\nand a target of mass $m_2$ at rest in the observer frame of the target, with\nprojectile energy $E$ and kinetic energy $T=E-m_1c^2$ in that frame, then\n\n$$E\\ \\ge\\ \\frac{c^2}{2m_2}\\Bigl[\\Bigl(\\sum_{j=1}^K\\mu_j\\Bigr)^2-m_1^2-m_2^2\\Bigr],\\qquad T\\ \\ge\\ \\frac{c^2}{2m_2}\\Bigl[\\Bigl(\\sum_{j=1}^K\\mu_j\\Bigr)^2-(m_1+m_2)^2\\Bigr],$$\n\nSeparately, under the same adopted balance, a single incoming massless particle\ncannot produce an outgoing state containing a massive particle. These are kinematic necessity statements under\nthe adopted conservation: they do not assert that any collision occurs, do\nnot fix probabilities, amplitudes or cross sections, and are not empirical\nfindings.\n\n**(f) Scope.** All clauses are conditional on the two postulates and on the\nstated idealisations; the conclusions carry the same idealisations. No\nmeasurement, precision, statistical or empirical claim is made.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "post-rpm-h-free-test-particle-action",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Let $I\\subset\\mathbb R$ be an open interval, and let $x:I\\to M$ be a $C^2$ future-directed timelike worldline on a prescribed smooth, time-oriented Lorentzian background $(M,g,T)$ of signature $(-+++)$, with $m>0$ a fixed primitive mass in kg and $c>0$ in m/s. For any compact segment $[a,b]\\subset I$ with $a<b$, adopt the stationary-action model on that segment\n\n$$S_{m,[a,b]}[x]=-mc\\int_a^b\\sqrt{-g(x',x') }\\,d\\lambda.$$\n\nHere $I\\subset\\mathbb R$ is an open interval, $\\lambda$ is its real coordinate, and $x'$ denotes $dx/d\\lambda$. The future-timelike condition makes $x'$ nowhere zero. On a neighborhood of $[a,b]$, $x$ is $C^2$ (so its tangent is $C^1$). If $\\widetilde\\lambda=f(\\lambda)$ is an increasing $C^1$ reparametrization with $f'>0$, then $\\sqrt{-g(dx/d\\widetilde\\lambda,dx/d\\widetilde\\lambda)}\\,d\\widetilde\\lambda=\\sqrt{-g(x',x')}\\,d\\lambda$ by the chain rule. An admissible variation is a one-parameter family $x_\\epsilon(\\lambda)$ that is $C^2$ jointly in $(\\epsilon,\\lambda)$, has fixed endpoint events $x_\\epsilon(a)=x(a)$ and $x_\\epsilon(b)=x(b)$, and remains future timelike for sufficiently small $|\\epsilon|$. The integrand is the proper-time length density, so $S_{m,[a,b]}=-mc^2\\int_a^b d\\tau$; its dimension is action (J s). The adopted premise concerns this ideal free test body only. It is neither a theorem about all matter nor an empirical report, and supplies no backreaction or completeness claim. The flat ideal-clock specialization agrees with [[post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light]]; adopting this action on a curved background remains a separate premise.\n\nThe cotangent phase-space carrier convention assumes countable choice ACω only; full choice is not assumed.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "post-rpm-h-massless-shell-action",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "On a prescribed background $(M,g,T)$, choose an open parameter interval $I$ and a compact segment $[a,b]\\subset I$ with $a<b$. In a spacetime chart containing the curve and sufficiently small variations, let $x:I\\to M$ be smooth near $[a,b]$, let $p(\\lambda)\\in T^*_{x(\\lambda)}M$ be a smooth covector field along $x$, and let $e$ be smooth and positive near $[a,b]$. In this chart, write $g_{ab}=g(\\partial_a,\\partial_b)$ for $a,b\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}$, let $(g^{ab})$ be the inverse matrix, and define $p^\\sharp=\\sum_{a,b=0}^3g^{ab}p_b\\partial_a$; equivalently, $g(p^\\sharp,w)=p(w)$ for every tangent vector $w$. Adopt the distinct massless classical particle action on this segment\n\n$$S_{0,[a,b]}[x,p,e]=\\int_a^b\\left[p_a x'^a-eC_0(x,p)\\right]d\\lambda,\\qquad C_0=\\tfrac12g^{ab}p_ap_b.$$\n\nAdmissible variations are smooth one-parameter families of chart fields $(x_\\epsilon,p_\\epsilon,e_\\epsilon)$ on a common neighborhood of $[a,b]$, with $x_\\epsilon(a)=x(a)$ and $x_\\epsilon(b)=x(b)$; the $p_\\epsilon$ and $e_\\epsilon$ variations are independent and have no endpoint constraints, while $e_\\epsilon>0$ for sufficiently small $|\\epsilon|$. Impose the physical branch only on stationary solutions after these variations: $p\\ne0$ and $p^\\sharp$ is future null. With all spacetime coordinates measured in metres and dimensionless $\\lambda$, $p_a$ has units kg m/s, $C_0$ has units kg²m²/s² and $e$ has units s/kg, making the action J s. Other parameter units scale $e$ accordingly. This is a separate adopted model: null curves have no proper-time parameter, the model does not arise by setting $m=0$ in $P=mU$, and its positive momentum scale is additional initial data. On the nonzero branch, the vertical differential is $d_pC_0=p^\\sharp\\ne0$: nondegeneracy of $g^{-1}$ and $p\\ne0$ give $p^\\sharp\\ne0$. Hence the shell is a regular primary constraint in the sense of [[def-rpm-h-regular-constraint-system]]. This postulate is not an empirical observation or a deduction from the massive model.\n\nThe cotangent phase-space carrier convention assumes countable choice ACω only; full choice is not assumed.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "D1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Use the inherited smooth bundle structure under [A1] and the quantity and temporal-shell conventions [F1,F2,F3]. Evaluate the covector on the normal: $p(n)=N^{-1}(p_0-\\beta^ip_i)=-\\kappa$ by [F3]. Thus $E_n=-cp(n)=c\\kappa>0$, while multiplying the shell root by $-c$ gives the chart Hamiltonian stated in [F3].",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "D2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "Differentiate $H=c(N\\kappa-\\beta^ip_i)$ with respect to $p_i$, keeping $t,q$ fixed. Since $\\partial\\kappa/\\partial p_i=h^{ij}p_j/\\kappa$, Hamilton's equation gives $\\dot q^i/c=Nh^{ij}p_j/\\kappa-\\beta^i$, so $w^i=h^{ij}p_j/\\kappa$. Its squared $h$-norm is $h^{ij}p_ip_j/(m^2c^2+h^{ij}p_ip_j)$, strictly below one for $m>0$ and equal to one for $m=0$ with nonzero spatial momentum.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "D3",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Cauchy–Schwarz in the positive definite metric $h$ gives $\\beta^ip_i\\le |\\beta|_h|p|_{h^{-1}}$, and $\\kappa\\ge |p|_{h^{-1}}$. If $\\beta^2<N^2$, then $|\\beta|_h<N$ and $N\\kappa-|\\beta|_h|p|_{h^{-1}}>0$ unless $p=0$; for $p=0,m>0$, $N\\kappa=Nmc>0$ as well, while the massless case excludes $p=0$. Hence $H>0$. If $\\beta^2>N^2$, choose $p_i$ a positive multiple of the metric dual of $\\beta$ and let that multiple grow. Then $\\beta^ip_i=|\\beta|_h|p|_{h^{-1}}$ and $H/c=N\\sqrt{m^2c^2+|p|_{h^{-1}}^2}-|\\beta|_h|p|_{h^{-1}}$ is negative for sufficiently large $|p|_{h^{-1}}$, because its ratio to $|p|_{h^{-1}}$ tends to $N-|\\beta|_h<0$. Step 1.1 still gives $E_n=c\\kappa>0$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "D4",
          "step": "2.2",
          "claim": "Along a solution, $dH/dt=H_t+H_{q^i}\\dot q^i+H_{p_i}\\dot p_i=H_t+H_{q^i}H_{p_i}-H_{p_i}H_{q^i}=H_t$. Thus explicit time independence suffices for conservation. More precisely, the value of $H$ along a connected solution interval is constant exactly when $H_t$ vanishes along that solution, by differentiating a constant in one direction and the mean value theorem in the other. This does not require $H_t$ to vanish at states off that solution.",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "D5",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "In a flat inertial chart with $N=1,\\beta=0,h=\\delta$, one has $H=c\\kappa=E_n$, agreeing with [F4]; this is the only use of the SR supplier.",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Statement — Coordinate Hamiltonian and normal-observer energy differ by shift: The conclusion concerns supplied local or finite data rather than an aggregate construction over an empty index set. Positive lapse and future-shell kappa make normal energy positive, with massive subluminal or nonzero-null unit normal speed. Spacelike coordinate time may give negative chart Hamiltonian; only explicit time independence yields its conservation.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Under the hypotheses of [[thm-rpm-h-temporal-shell-reduction]], all lapse, shift and spatial-metric coefficients in the reduced expressions below are evaluated at the spacetime event $(ct,q)$, as in the temporal-shell supplier; $n$ is the normal at that same event. The future unit normal $n=N^{-1}(\\partial_0-\\beta^i\\partial_i)$ measures positive particle energy\n\n$$E_n=-c\\,p(n)=c\\kappa,$$\n\nwhile the chart's reduced Hamiltonian is $H=c(N\\kappa-\\beta^ip_i)$. The normal-frame spatial velocity is $w^i=(\\dot q^i/c+\\beta^i)/N=h^{ij}p_j/\\kappa$, with $h_{ij}w^iw^j<1$ for $m>0$ and $=1$ for a nonzero massless state. Write $|\\beta|_h:=\\sqrt{h_{ij}\\beta^i\\beta^j}$ and $|p|_{h^{-1}}:=\\sqrt{h^{ij}p_ip_j}$. If $\\beta^2:=h_{ij}\\beta^i\\beta^j<N^2$, then $H>0$. If $\\beta^2>N^2$, $H$ can be negative even though $E_n>0$. Along a Hamiltonian solution, $dH/dt=\\partial_tH$; explicit time independence is sufficient for its conservation. On a connected solution interval, $H(t,q(t),p(t))$ is constant if and only if $\\partial_tH(t,q(t),p(t))=0$ throughout that interval.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "5477ebe9f2496a3c4f1d3b18ba7a773ad5b49792e0d047a9079fe4e5b56b633e",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement — Coordinate Hamiltonian and normal-observer energy differ by shift: The permitted zero values and nonzero normalization domain are fixed by the declared input conditions. Positive lapse and future-shell kappa make normal energy positive, with massive subluminal or nonzero-null unit normal speed. Spacelike coordinate time may give negative chart Hamiltonian; only explicit time independence yields its conservation.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Under the hypotheses of [[thm-rpm-h-temporal-shell-reduction]], all lapse, shift and spatial-metric coefficients in the reduced expressions below are evaluated at the spacetime event $(ct,q)$, as in the temporal-shell supplier; $n$ is the normal at that same event. The future unit normal $n=N^{-1}(\\partial_0-\\beta^i\\partial_i)$ measures positive particle energy\n\n$$E_n=-c\\,p(n)=c\\kappa,$$\n\nwhile the chart's reduced Hamiltonian is $H=c(N\\kappa-\\beta^ip_i)$. The normal-frame spatial velocity is $w^i=(\\dot q^i/c+\\beta^i)/N=h^{ij}p_j/\\kappa$, with $h_{ij}w^iw^j<1$ for $m>0$ and $=1$ for a nonzero massless state. Write $|\\beta|_h:=\\sqrt{h_{ij}\\beta^i\\beta^j}$ and $|p|_{h^{-1}}:=\\sqrt{h^{ij}p_ip_j}$. If $\\beta^2:=h_{ij}\\beta^i\\beta^j<N^2$, then $H>0$. If $\\beta^2>N^2$, $H$ can be negative even though $E_n>0$. Along a Hamiltonian solution, $dH/dt=\\partial_tH$; explicit time independence is sufficient for its conservation. On a connected solution interval, $H(t,q(t),p(t))$ is constant if and only if $\\partial_tH(t,q(t),p(t))=0$ throughout that interval.",
