          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Under the hypotheses of [[thm-rpm-h-temporal-shell-reduction]], all lapse, shift and spatial-metric coefficients in the reduced expressions below are evaluated at the spacetime event $(ct,q)$, as in the temporal-shell supplier; $n$ is the normal at that same event. The future unit normal $n=N^{-1}(\\partial_0-\\beta^i\\partial_i)$ measures positive particle energy\n\n$$E_n=-c\\,p(n)=c\\kappa,$$\n\nwhile the chart's reduced Hamiltonian is $H=c(N\\kappa-\\beta^ip_i)$. The normal-frame spatial velocity is $w^i=(\\dot q^i/c+\\beta^i)/N=h^{ij}p_j/\\kappa$, with $h_{ij}w^iw^j<1$ for $m>0$ and $=1$ for a nonzero massless state. Write $|\\beta|_h:=\\sqrt{h_{ij}\\beta^i\\beta^j}$ and $|p|_{h^{-1}}:=\\sqrt{h^{ij}p_ip_j}$. If $\\beta^2:=h_{ij}\\beta^i\\beta^j<N^2$, then $H>0$. If $\\beta^2>N^2$, $H$ can be negative even though $E_n>0$. Along a Hamiltonian solution, $dH/dt=\\partial_tH$; explicit time independence is sufficient for its conservation. On a connected solution interval, $H(t,q(t),p(t))$ is constant if and only if $\\partial_tH(t,q(t),p(t))=0$ throughout that interval.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "5477ebe9f2496a3c4f1d3b18ba7a773ad5b49792e0d047a9079fe4e5b56b633e",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
