      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "reviewer": "alpha-batch-25",
        "notes": "Confirmed refuter:25:2. For N=1,h=δ,β=(0,sin(t/t0),0), H depends on t off p2=0, but Hamilton evolution with p=(p1,0,0) has constant p and constant H. The corrected statement makes time independence sufficient; dH/dt=H_t gives conservation exactly when H_t vanishes along that solution interval. Pairing with the future normal gives E_n=cκ>0; differentiation gives w=h-inverse p/κ and its massive/null norm bounds. Positive h bounds β·p and proves H>0 for |β|<N; aligned large p proves negativity possible for |β|>N. At |β|=N the statement makes no additional strict-positivity claim. SR is used only in the flat specialization; no observed inputs occur."
