    "ex-rpm-h-shifted-chart-negative-energy": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-rpm-h-temporal-chart-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice ACω, as in [[def-countable-choice]], for the smooth cotangent-bundle carrier convention used by this page; no full Axiom of Choice is assumed.\n\nLet $(M,g,T)$ be a smooth, time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold of signature $(-+++)$, with the geometric meaning fixed by [[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]. Here $T$ denotes the future-cone field called $N$ in that definition. Let $I\\subset\\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open interval, let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ be open, and let $U\\subset M$ be open. A **temporal product chart** is a smooth diffeomorphism $\\chi:U\\to I\\times\\Omega$. Its coordinate functions $x^0,x^1,x^2,x^3$ are the four component functions of $\\chi$. Spatial indices $i,j$ range over $1,2,3$, spacetime indices $a,b$ range over $0,1,2,3$, and repeated upper/lower indices are summed. We require $x^0$ and each $x^i$ to have length units in the physical interpretation.\n\nThe chart has **lapse-shift data** $(N,\\beta,h)$ when $N:I\\times\\Omega\\to(0,\\infty)$, $\\beta^i:I\\times\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$, and $h_{ij}:I\\times\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ are smooth, $h_{ij}=h_{ji}$, and the matrix $(h_{ij})$ is positive definite at each point, with\n\n$$g=-N^2(dx^0)^2+h_{ij}(dx^i+\\beta^i dx^0)(dx^j+\\beta^j dx^0).$$\n\nWrite $h^{ij}$ for the inverse matrix. The normal field is\n\n$$n=N^{-1}(\\partial_0-\\beta^i\\partial_i).$$\n\nDirect contraction gives $g(n,n)=-1$; require that $n$ is future-directed in the supplied time orientation. This field of observers is distinct from the coordinate vector $\\partial_0$, which may be spacelike. The coefficients $N,\\beta^i,h_{ij}$ are dimensionless when all four coordinates are in metres. This definition is local to the specified product chart; it asserts no global temporal foliation.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-rpm-h-temporal-shell-reduction",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $m\\ge0$ and $c>0$. Assume a temporal product chart with lapse-shift data $(N,\\beta,h)$ as in [[def-rpm-h-temporal-chart-data]], all four coordinates measured in metres. Write $g_{ab}=g(\\partial_a,\\partial_b)$ for $a,b\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}$ and let $g^{ab}$ denote the entries of the inverse matrix; then $p^\\sharp:=\\sum_{a,b=0}^3g^{ab}p_b\\partial_a$ is the unique vector satisfying $g(p^\\sharp,w)=p(w)$ for every tangent vector $w$. Put $T=\\{t\\in\\mathbb R:ct\\in I\\}$. In every reduced expression below, $N$, $\\beta^i$, $h_{ij}$ and $h^{ij}$ mean their pullbacks to $T\\times\\Omega$: $N(ct,q)$, $\\beta^i(ct,q)$, $h_{ij}(ct,q)$ and $h^{ij}(ct,q)$, respectively. Thus $\\kappa$, $p_0$ and $H$ are functions of $(t,q,p)$ on the massive reduced domain, or on its nonzero-spatial-momentum part when $m=0$; the spacetime metric and shell covector are evaluated at $(ct,q)$. On the future shell $C_m=\\tfrac12(g^{ab}p_ap_b+m^2c^2)=0$, define\n\n$$\\kappa=\\sqrt{m^2c^2+h^{ij}p_ip_j},\\quad p_0=\\beta^ip_i-N\\kappa,\\quad H(t,q,p)=-cp_0=c(N\\kappa-\\beta^ip_i).$$\n\nFor $m=0$, require $p_i\\ne0$. Let $J=[t_1,t_2]$ satisfy $t_1<t_2$ and $[ct_1,ct_2]\\subset I$, where $I$ is the first-coordinate interval of the temporal product chart; require the path $t\\mapsto(ct,q(t))$ to stay in the chart. Take $q^i,p_i$ smooth on a neighborhood of $J$ and vary them by smooth one-parameter families that keep the paths in the chart, fix $q$ at both endpoints and vary $p$ freely; in the massless case the paths and sufficiently small variations stay in $p_i\\ne0$. Then the gauge $x^0=ct$ reduces the first-order action to $\\int_{t_1}^{t_2}(p_i\\dot q^i-H)dt$, and its variational equations on the interior of $J$ are exactly $\\dot q^i=H_{p_i}$, $\\dot p_i=-H_{q^i}$. The construction is local to this chart and intervals transverse to its time slices; no global curved-space gauge or quotient is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-rpm-h-background-and-model-quantities",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice ACω, as in [[def-countable-choice]], for the smooth cotangent-bundle carrier convention used by this page; no full Axiom of Choice is assumed.\n\nFix a prescribed smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold $(M,g,T)$ with signature $(-+++)$, as in [[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]. Here $T$ denotes the supplied smooth timelike field selecting the future cone, called $N$ in that geometric definition. Use the distinction among a spacetime, a worldline, a pointwise observer, an observer congruence, a tetrad and a chart given in [[def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline]]. This definition does not make $g$ dynamical.\n\nAt each $x\\in M$, define $v^\\flat:=g_x(v,\\cdot)$ for $v\\in T_xM$. Nondegeneracy of $g_x$ makes $\\flat_x:T_xM\\to T_x^*M$ an isomorphism; write $p^\\sharp:=\\flat_x^{-1}(p)$ for $p\\in T_x^*M$, and define $g_x^{-1}(p,r):=g_x(p^\\sharp,r^\\sharp)$. In a chart, if $g_{ab}:=g(\\partial_a,\\partial_b)$ and $(g^{ab})$ is its inverse matrix, then $p^\\sharp=\\sum_{a,b=0}^3g^{ab}p_b\\partial_a$. A covector $p$ is **future causal** here exactly when $p^\\sharp$ is a nonzero future-causal vector in the sense of [[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]].\n\nIn this relativistic-particle model, $m>0$ is a fixed **primitive mass parameter** measured in kilograms, and $c>0$ is a fixed **primitive conversion parameter** measured in metres per second. They are parameters of the adopted physical formulation, not quantities derived by this definition. Coordinates $x^a$ in an SI chart have length units; a cotangent vector $p=p_a\\,dx^a$ has units of momentum, $\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$, when all four coordinates are in metres. The canonical one-form $p_a\\,dx^a$ therefore has action units $\\mathrm{J\\,s}$. Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval with nonempty interior and let $\\gamma:I\\to M$ be a $C^1$ curve; let $\\lambda$ be its real parameter and write $V=d\\gamma/d\\lambda$. When $V$ is future timelike, set $s:=\\sqrt{-g(V,V)}$ and define the proper-time density by $d\\tau=s\\,d\\lambda/c$. The tangent is then nonzero. If $\\widetilde\\lambda=f(\\lambda)$ is an increasing $C^1$ reparametrization with $f'(\\lambda)>0$, then $s_{\\widetilde\\lambda}=s_\\lambda/f'(\\lambda)$, so $s_{\\widetilde\\lambda}\\,d\\widetilde\\lambda=s_\\lambda\\,d\\lambda$. The length action $-mc\\int s\\,d\\lambda$ has action units. For a future unit observer $n$ and future causal covector $p$, define the model normal-observer energy and spatial momentum covector by\n\n$$E_n(p):=-c\\,p(n),\\qquad q_n(p):=p+p(n)n^\\flat,$$\n\nwhere $n^\\flat=g(n,\\cdot)$. We have $q_n(p)(n)=0$; write $n^\\perp:=\\{v\\in T_xM:g_x(v,n)=0\\}$. To verify the norm convention, choose a $g_x$-orthonormal basis $(e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ with $e_0$ future-directed (replace $e_0$ by $-e_0$ if necessary), and write $n=n^0e_0+\\sum_{i=1}^3n^ie_i$ and $v=v^0e_0+\\sum_{i=1}^3v^ie_i$. Use $n_s\\cdot v_s:=\\sum_{i=1}^3n^iv^i$, $|n_s|^2:=\\sum_{i=1}^3(n^i)^2$, and $|v_s|^2:=\\sum_{i=1}^3(v^i)^2$. The future-cone convention gives $n^0>|n_s|$, and $g_x(n,n)=-1$ gives $(n^0)^2=1+|n_s|^2$. If $g_x(v,n)=0$, then $v^0=(n_s\\cdot v_s)/n^0$, so\n\n$$g_x(v,v)=|v_s|^2-\\frac{(n_s\\cdot v_s)^2}{(n^0)^2}\\ge\\frac{|v_s|^2}{(n^0)^2}.$$\n\nHere $|n_s|^2|v_s|^2-(n_s\\cdot v_s)^2=\\sum_{1\\le i<j\\le3}(n^iv^j-n^jv^i)^2\\ge0$; if $v_s=0$, orthogonality also gives $v^0=0$. Thus $g_x$ is positive definite on $n^\\perp$, and the isomorphism $\\sharp_x$ carries covectors annihilating $n$ onto $n^\\perp$, so $g_x^{-1}$ is positive definite on that covector subspace. Define $\\|q_n(p)\\|_n:=\\sqrt{g_x^{-1}(q_n(p),q_n(p))}$. These are quantity definitions and unit conventions; they do not identify canonical momentum with mechanical momentum, assert that an apparatus measured these values, or establish a dynamical law.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Use the inherited smooth bundle structure under [A1] and the temporal-chart data [F1]. The coordinate transformation is invertible with determinant one and pulls back the Minkowski line element $-(dX^0)^2+(dX^1)^2+(dX^2)^2+(dX^3)^2$ to $g$, proving flatness. The lapse is positive and $h=\\delta$ is positive definite, so the chart satisfies [F1]; $\\partial_0$ is spacelike since $g(\\partial_0,\\partial_0)=-1+4=3>0$, while $dx^0$ is temporal.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Here $\\kappa=\\sqrt{m^2c^2+p_1^2}$, so [F2] gives $H=c(\\kappa-2p_1)$. The inequality $p_1>mc/\\sqrt3$ implies $m^2c^2<3p_1^2$, hence $\\kappa^2=m^2c^2+p_1^2<4p_1^2$, so $\\kappa<2p_1$ and $H<0$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Here $n=\\partial_0-2\\partial_1$, so $p(n)=p_0-2p_1=-\\kappa$ on the future root; [F3] gives $E_n=-cp(n)=c\\kappa>0$. The shell velocity formula gives $|w|_h=p_1/\\kappa<1$, since $m>0$. The coordinate equation from [F2] gives $\\dot q^1/c=p_1/\\kappa-2$; because $\\kappa^2<4p_1^2$, $p_1/\\kappa>1/2$, so $2-p_1/\\kappa>1$. Thus coordinate speed has magnitude greater than $c$ while local observer speed is less than $c$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
